{
  id:"co-humerus-elbow-fractures", title:"Upper limb fractures and joint injuries: shaft of humerus, supracondylar fracture and elbow dislocation", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Arm and elbow", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["fracture shaft of humerus","supracondylar fracture humerus","elbow dislocation","olecranon fracture","radial head fracture","upper limb fractures"],
  simple:"The arm bone (humerus) can break in its middle part, which may injure the radial nerve and cause wrist drop. In children, a fall on the hand often breaks the bone just above the elbow (supracondylar fracture), which can damage the artery. The elbow can also dislocate, and it stiffens quickly, so careful active exercise is essential.",
  flow:["Fall on outstretched hand or direct blow","Shaft humerus, supracondylar, olecranon, radial head or elbow dislocation","Pain, swelling, deformity, check radial, median nerve and pulse","X-ray AP and lateral; three-point bony relationship at elbow","Brace, cast, K-wires, plating or reduction","Active elbow and shoulder range, strength, function; avoid forced stretch"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Fracture shaft of humerus", p:["Cause: direct blow or fall; spiral fracture from twisting.","Complication: radial nerve palsy at the spiral groove (wrist drop), mostly neuropraxia and recovers.","Management: U-slab then functional brace (Sarmiento) for most; plating or nailing for open, unstable, bilateral or polytrauma.","Physio: pendulum exercises, elbow, wrist and hand range from early, isometrics; cock-up splint if wrist drop; full shoulder range after union (about 8-12 weeks)."]},
    {h:"Supracondylar fracture of humerus (children)", p:["Commonest elbow fracture in children aged 5-10; extension type (about 95 percent) from fall on outstretched hand.","Gartland classification: I undisplaced, II displaced with posterior cortex intact, III completely displaced.","Complications: brachial artery injury and Volkmann's ischaemic contracture, median or anterior interosseous nerve injury, malunion causing cubitus varus (gunstock deformity), myositis ossificans, stiffness.","Management: above-elbow slab for type I; closed reduction and percutaneous K-wires for II-III.","Physio: watch for compartment syndrome; after removal of cast at about 3-4 weeks, gentle active elbow flexion-extension and forearm rotation; NO passive stretching or massage."]},
    {h:"Elbow dislocation", p:["Usually posterior or posterolateral after fall on outstretched hand; may be with radial head or coronoid fracture (terrible triad).","Clinical: three-point bony relationship (olecranon and two epicondyles) is disturbed; in supracondylar fracture it is maintained.","Management: closed reduction, short splint 5-10 days, early active movement within stable arc."]},
    {h:"Other elbow injuries", p:["Olecranon fracture: tension band wiring; early active movement.","Radial head fracture (adults): Mason types I-IV; type I early movement, others fixation or replacement.","Lateral condyle fracture in children: needs accurate fixation (risk of non-union and cubitus valgus with tardy ulnar palsy)."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy principles", p:["Immobilisation: shoulder and finger exercises, isometrics, elevation, oedema control.","Mobilisation: active and active-assisted elbow range, hold-relax, forearm rotation, gravity-assisted positions; aim for functional arc of about 30-130 degrees.","Strengthening of biceps, triceps and grip; functional tasks (eating, combing).","Static progressive or dynamic splints for stubborn stiffness; avoid forceful passive stretching."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Elbow injuries stiffen easily and complications like VIC and myositis ossificans are serious; careful monitoring and active exercise give the best result."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw the posterior elbow with olecranon tip and two epicondyles forming an isosceles triangle at 90 degrees flexion; show it disturbed in dislocation and maintained in supracondylar fracture.",
  evidence:[["Functional bracing for humeral shaft fractures","High union rates for most closed fractures","Moderate"],["Early motion after simple elbow dislocation","Better range, no increase in instability","Moderate"],["K-wire fixation for displaced supracondylar fracture","Lower malunion and cubitus varus","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Sarmiento functional bracing studies; AAOS guideline on paediatric supracondylar fractures",
  outcomes:[["Goniometry","Elbow flexion-extension and forearm rotation"],["Carrying angle","Malunion (cubitus varus)"],["QuickDASH","Upper limb function"],["Grip dynamometer","Hand strength"]],
  recent:["The AAOS clinical practice guideline supports closed reduction and pinning for displaced paediatric supracondylar fractures."],
  mnemonic:{word:"RAM", items:[["R","Radial nerve: shaft humerus"],["A","Artery (brachial): supracondylar"],["M","Myositis ossificans: elbow, no massage"]], extra:"Three-point bony relationship is disturbed only in dislocation."},
  example:"A 9-year-old girl in Kanchipuram falls from a tree and has a Gartland III supracondylar fracture. After K-wiring, the physio checks finger movement and pulses, and at 4 weeks starts gentle active elbow exercises with games.",
  flags:["Pain on passive finger extension or absent radial pulse","New wrist drop after manipulation or plating","Increasing elbow stiffness with warm swelling","Cubitus varus developing after union"],
  viva:[["Nerve injured in shaft humerus fracture?","Radial nerve in the spiral groove."],["Deformity after malunited supracondylar fracture?","Cubitus varus (gunstock deformity)."],["How do you differentiate supracondylar fracture from elbow dislocation clinically?","The three-point bony relationship is maintained in supracondylar fracture and disturbed in dislocation."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Fractures of the upper limb"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Injuries around the elbow."},{b:"brotzman",ch:"Elbow injuries"}],
  keywords:"humerus shaft supracondylar fracture elbow dislocation olecranon radial head cubitus varus",
  mcq:[["Gunstock deformity follows malunion of:",["Colles fracture","Supracondylar fracture of humerus","Olecranon fracture","Scaphoid fracture"],1,"Malunion produces cubitus varus, called gunstock deformity."],["In posterior elbow dislocation, the three-point bony relationship is:",["Maintained","Disturbed","Unaffected by flexion","Not testable"],1,"Dislocation disturbs the relationship of olecranon and epicondyles."]]
},
{
  id:"co-forearm-fractures", title:"Forearm fractures: both bones, Monteggia and Galeazzi fracture-dislocations", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Forearm", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["both bone forearm fracture","Monteggia fracture","Galeazzi fracture","radius ulna fracture","night stick fracture","forearm fracture"],
  simple:"The forearm has two bones, radius and ulna, joined at both ends so that the forearm can turn palm up and down. When one bone breaks and shortens, the joint at the other end often dislocates. Monteggia is a broken ulna with a dislocated radial head; Galeazzi is a broken radius with a dislocated lower ulna.",
  flow:["Fall or direct blow on the forearm","Fracture of radius, ulna or both, with joint dislocation","Pain, deformity, loss of rotation; check posterior interosseous nerve","X-ray whole forearm including elbow and wrist","Adults: open reduction and plating; children: closed reduction and cast","Restore pronation-supination, grip and function"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Anatomy and principle", p:["Radius and ulna form a ring with the proximal and distal radioulnar joints; a fracture of one bone with shortening usually means injury to a joint (like breaking a ring in two places).","Always X-ray elbow and wrist with a forearm fracture."]},
    {h:"Types", p:["Both-bone fractures: common in children (greenstick) and adults (displaced).","Isolated ulnar shaft (night stick) fracture from direct blow while protecting the head.","Monteggia: fracture of proximal ulna with dislocation of radial head (Bado types I-IV, type I anterior commonest); posterior interosseous nerve may be injured.","Galeazzi: fracture of distal third radius with dislocation of distal radioulnar joint; called 'fracture of necessity' as it needs surgery in adults."]},
    {h:"Clinical features and investigations", p:["Pain, swelling, deformity, loss of rotation, tender radial head or prominent ulnar head.","Check for finger drop (PIN palsy) and compartment syndrome.","X-ray AP and lateral of the entire forearm; radiocapitellar line should pass through the capitulum in all views."]},
    {h:"Management", p:["Children: closed reduction and above-elbow cast 6 weeks; elastic nails if unstable.","Adults: open reduction and internal fixation with compression plates to restore length, alignment and the radial bow; DRUJ or radial head reduced and stabilised.","Union usually 8-12 weeks in adults."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy", p:["Post-op: elevation, finger and shoulder exercises from day 1; elbow and wrist active range once wound settles if fixation is stable.","Gentle active pronation and supination early, then active-assisted; avoid heavy lifting and forced rotation until union.","Strengthening: grip putty, wrist curls, forearm rotation with hammer lever (3 x 10-15), functional and work tasks.","Joint mobilisation of radioulnar joints after union for persistent rotation loss."]},
    {h:"Complications and conclusion", p:["Malunion with loss of rotation, non-union, radioulnar synostosis, PIN palsy, compartment syndrome, refracture after plate removal.","Anatomical reduction and early guided motion preserve forearm rotation, which is vital for daily activities."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw radius and ulna side by side. For Monteggia show a break in the upper ulna and the radial head displaced; for Galeazzi show a break in the lower radius and the ulnar head displaced at the wrist.",
  evidence:[["Open reduction and plating of adult both-bone fractures","High union rate and good rotation","Moderate"],["Early active motion after stable plating","Better rotation and function","Moderate"],["Closed treatment of paediatric forearm fractures","Good outcomes due to remodelling","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Standard orthopaedic trauma texts and observational series",
  outcomes:[["Goniometry","Pronation and supination"],["Grip strength","Hand function"],["PRWE or QuickDASH","Upper limb disability"],["Radiograph","Union and radiocapitellar alignment"]],
  recent:["Elastic stable intramedullary nailing is increasingly used for unstable forearm fractures in older children."],
  mnemonic:{word:"MUGR", items:[["M","Monteggia"],["U","Ulna broken"],["G","Galeazzi"],["R","Radius broken"]], extra:"MU-GR: Monteggia-Ulna, Galeazzi-Radius."},
  example:"A 32-year-old auto driver in Madurai falls from his vehicle and sustains a Galeazzi fracture. After plating, the physio starts finger, elbow and gentle forearm rotation exercises and later grip strengthening for his return to driving.",
  flags:["Finger drop after Monteggia injury (posterior interosseous nerve)","Tense, painful forearm (compartment syndrome)","Persistent loss of rotation (malunion or synostosis)","Pain and movement at fracture after 3-4 months (non-union)"],
  viva:[["What is Monteggia fracture?","Fracture of the proximal ulna with dislocation of the radial head."],["Why is Galeazzi called fracture of necessity?","It needs open reduction and internal fixation in adults for a good result."],["Which nerve is at risk in Monteggia?","Posterior interosseous nerve."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Fractures of the forearm"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Injuries of the forearm and wrist."},{b:"brotzman",ch:"Hand and wrist injuries"}],
  keywords:"forearm fracture both bones Monteggia Galeazzi radius ulna pronation supination",
  mcq:[["Galeazzi fracture involves:",["Proximal ulna and radial head dislocation","Distal radius shaft and DRUJ dislocation","Both bones without dislocation","Scaphoid and lunate"],1,"Galeazzi is a distal third radius fracture with DRUJ dislocation."],["After forearm fracture, which movement is most important to restore for daily function?",["Shoulder abduction","Pronation and supination","Thumb opposition","Finger abduction"],1,"Forearm rotation is the special function of the radioulnar joints."]]
},
{
  id:"co-hip-femur-fractures", title:"Lower limb fractures: neck of femur, intertrochanteric and shaft of femur", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Hip and thigh", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["fracture neck of femur","intertrochanteric fracture","shaft of femur fracture","hip fracture","Garden classification","hemiarthroplasty"],
  simple:"Older people with weak bones often break the top of the thigh bone after a simple fall. Fractures of the neck can cut off the blood supply to the femoral head, so they are often treated by replacing the head; fractures lower down (intertrochanteric) are fixed with screws and plates or nails. Young people break the femoral shaft in road accidents and need nailing.",
  flow:["Low-energy fall in elderly or high-energy trauma in young","Neck, trochanteric or shaft fracture of femur","Pain, cannot bear weight, shortened externally rotated limb","X-ray, classify (Garden, Boyd-Griffin), check general health","Hemiarthroplasty, screws, DHS or nail; early surgery","Early mobilisation, gait training, falls prevention"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Fracture neck of femur", p:["Intracapsular; commonest in elderly osteoporotic women.","Garden classification: I incomplete or impacted valgus, II complete undisplaced, III complete partially displaced, IV completely displaced.","Complications: avascular necrosis of head (retinacular vessels torn) and non-union; in young high-energy cases urgent fixation is needed.","Treatment: Garden I-II: cannulated screws; III-IV in elderly: hemiarthroplasty (Austin Moore or bipolar) or total hip replacement in fit active patients."]},
    {h:"Intertrochanteric fracture", p:["Extracapsular, good blood supply, so AVN and non-union are rare, but malunion in varus and shortening can occur.","Clinical: more shortening and external rotation (up to 90 degrees) than neck fractures; marked bruising.","Treatment: dynamic hip screw (DHS) or proximal femoral nail (PFN) for unstable patterns."]},
    {h:"Fracture shaft of femur", p:["High-energy trauma in young adults; blood loss may be 1-1.5 litres; watch for fat embolism.","Treatment: interlocking intramedullary nail (adults); children: Pavlik or gallows traction (under 2 years), spica cast, elastic nails.","Thomas splint for first aid and traction."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy after hip fracture surgery", p:["Day 0-1: breathing exercises, ankle pumps, quadriceps and gluteal isometrics, sitting out of bed.","Gait: walker then sticks; weight bearing as tolerated after arthroplasty and most stable fixations, protected after screw fixation in young patients.","Hip precautions after posterior approach hemiarthroplasty (no flexion beyond 90, no adduction across midline, no internal rotation) for about 6 weeks.","Strengthening of hip abductors and extensors, quadriceps (3 x 10), sit-to-stand, balance and stairs; home safety and falls prevention."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy after femoral shaft nailing", p:["Quadriceps setting, straight leg raise, knee flexion to 90 degrees within the first 2 weeks, prevent quadriceps adhesions.","Weight bearing as allowed by the surgeon (often early with stable locked nails), progressive strengthening and gait."]},
    {h:"Complications and conclusion", p:["Pressure sores, pneumonia, DVT, UTI, confusion, AVN, non-union, malunion, knee stiffness, limb length discrepancy.","Early surgery and early mobilisation reduce mortality in hip fracture; physiotherapy aims at independent walking and falls prevention."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw the proximal femur with a line through the neck (intracapsular) and another between the trochanters (extracapsular). Shade the retinacular vessels along the neck to show why neck fractures risk AVN.",
  evidence:[["Surgery within 36-48 h for hip fracture","Lower mortality and complications","Moderate"],["Early mobilisation after hip fracture surgery","Better walking and fewer complications","Moderate"],["Extended progressive resistance and balance training","Improves mobility after hip fracture","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"NICE CG124 Hip fracture management; Cochrane reviews on mobilisation after hip fracture",
  outcomes:[["Timed Up and Go","Mobility and fall risk"],["Harris Hip Score","Hip function"],["New Mobility Score","Pre-fracture and post-fracture mobility"],["Berg Balance Scale","Balance"]],
  recent:["NICE hip fracture guidance recommends surgery on the day of or day after admission and mobilisation by the day after surgery."],
  mnemonic:{word:"NIS", items:[["N","Neck: AVN risk, replace"],["I","Intertrochanteric: fix with DHS or nail"],["S","Shaft: nail, watch fat embolism"]], extra:"Garden I-IV: higher number, more displacement."},
  example:"A 72-year-old woman in Pondicherry slips in the bathroom and has a Garden IV neck of femur fracture. After bipolar hemiarthroplasty she stands with a walker the next day and learns hip precautions and a safe home layout.",
  flags:["Breathlessness, confusion, petechiae after femoral shaft fracture (fat embolism)","Calf swelling (DVT)","Sudden pain, shortening and rotation after hemiarthroplasty (dislocation)","Pressure sore over sacrum or heel"],
  viva:[["Why does fracture neck femur have high AVN risk?","The retinacular vessels supplying the head run along the neck and are torn."],["Attitude of limb in intertrochanteric fracture?","Marked shortening with external rotation."],["Treatment of Garden IV in a 75-year-old?","Hemiarthroplasty or total hip replacement."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Fractures of the lower limb"},{c:"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence. Hip fracture: management (CG124). London: NICE; 2011, updated.", u:"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg124"},{b:"brotzman",ch:"Hip fractures"}],
  keywords:"neck of femur intertrochanteric shaft femur Garden hemiarthroplasty DHS nail hip fracture",
  mcq:[["Which hip fracture has the highest risk of avascular necrosis?",["Intertrochanteric","Subtrochanteric","Displaced intracapsular neck","Greater trochanter avulsion"],2,"Displaced neck fractures tear the retinacular vessels."],["Fat embolism is most associated with:",["Clavicle fracture","Shaft of femur fracture","Scaphoid fracture","Patella fracture"],1,"Long bone fractures, especially femur, release marrow fat."]]
},
{
  id:"co-knee-leg-injuries", title:"Lower limb fractures and joint injuries: patella, tibial plateau, tibial shaft and knee ligament and meniscus injuries", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Knee and leg", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["fracture patella","tibial plateau fracture","fracture tibia","meniscus tear","collateral ligament injury","bumper fracture","knee injuries"],
  simple:"Around the knee, the kneecap can break from a fall or sudden quadriceps pull, the upper tibia can crack from a car bumper, and the shin can break in road accidents. Twisting injuries tear the meniscus or knee ligaments. All need careful quadriceps work and knee bending exercise to avoid a stiff, weak knee.",
  flow:["Fall, direct blow, twisting or high-energy accident","Patella, tibial plateau or shaft fracture; meniscus or ligament tear","Pain, effusion, locking, giving way, inability to extend","X-ray, CT for plateau, MRI for soft tissue","Fixation (tension band, plates, nail) or cast; repair or reconstruct","Quadriceps, knee range, proprioception and gait"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Fracture patella", p:["Transverse from sudden quadriceps contraction (indirect), comminuted from direct blow.","Undisplaced with intact extensor mechanism (can do SLR): cylinder cast 4-6 weeks.","Displaced: tension band wiring; patellectomy for severe comminution (weakens quadriceps).","Physio: SLR and isometrics early, knee flexion progressed as per fixation (often 0-90 degrees by 6 weeks), avoid extensor lag."]},
    {h:"Tibial plateau fracture", p:["Bumper fracture: valgus force on lateral plateau; Schatzker types I-VI.","Treatment: non-operative for undisplaced; ORIF with buttress plates and bone graft for depressed or split fractures.","Physio: early CPM or active knee range, non-weight bearing or touch weight bearing 8-12 weeks, quadriceps strengthening."]},
    {h:"Tibial shaft fractures", p:["Commonest long bone fracture, often open due to subcutaneous position.","Treatment: cast (stable closed), interlocking nail (most adults), external fixator (open contaminated).","Complications: compartment syndrome, delayed union and non-union (poor blood supply in lower third), infection, malunion.","Physio: toe and ankle movements, quadriceps and SLR, knee and hip range, graded weight bearing with crutches."]},
    {h:"Knee ligament and meniscus injuries", p:["MCL: valgus stress; grade I-II treated with hinged brace and exercise; LCL with posterolateral corner injury may need surgery.","ACL and PCL: anterior and posterior drawer, Lachman test; reconstruction for unstable active patients.","Meniscus tear: twisting on a flexed weight-bearing knee; joint line tenderness, McMurray and Apley grind tests, locking; partial meniscectomy or repair.","Unhappy triad (O'Donoghue): ACL, MCL and meniscus."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy principles around the knee", p:["Control effusion (ice, compression, elevation) since effusion inhibits quadriceps.","Restore full extension first, then flexion; patellar mobilisation.","Quadriceps: setting, SLR, closed chain mini squats, step ups, leg press (3 x 10-15).","Proprioception, balance board, gait re-education; return to sport criteria based on strength and hop tests."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Knee injuries need early quadriceps activation, full extension and protected loading; stiffness and quadriceps weakness are the commonest avoidable problems."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a front view of the knee showing the patella, tibial plateau (with a depressed lateral fragment), menisci as C-shapes and collateral ligaments. Label each injury site.",
  evidence:[["Exercise therapy vs arthroscopic partial meniscectomy for degenerative tears","Similar outcomes; exercise first","High"],["Intramedullary nailing for adult tibial shaft fractures","High union and early function","Moderate"],["Early quadriceps and range exercise after knee fractures","Reduces stiffness and extensor lag","Moderate (clinical consensus)"]],
  evidenceSource:"Randomised trials of meniscectomy vs exercise (e.g. Kise et al., BMJ 2016) and orthopaedic trauma texts",
  outcomes:[["Goniometry","Knee range"],["Extensor lag","Quadriceps control"],["Lysholm or KOOS","Knee function"],["Single leg hop test","Return to sport readiness"]],
  recent:["Trials such as Kise et al. (BMJ 2016) found exercise therapy as good as arthroscopic partial meniscectomy for degenerative meniscal tears in middle-aged patients."],
  mnemonic:{word:"PPST", items:[["P","Patella: tension band wiring"],["P","Plateau: bumper fracture"],["S","Shaft: nail, watch compartment"],["T","Tears: ligament and meniscus"]], extra:"Unhappy triad: ACL, MCL, medial meniscus."},
  example:"A 26-year-old bike rider in Erode has a transverse patella fracture treated with tension band wiring. The physio starts quadriceps setting and SLR on day 1 and progresses knee flexion steadily, avoiding extensor lag.",
  flags:["Tense calf with pain on passive toe stretch (compartment syndrome)","Locked knee unable to fully extend (displaced bucket handle tear)","Foot drop after knee injury (common peroneal nerve)","Wound discharge over implant (infection)"],
  viva:[["Treatment of displaced transverse patella fracture?","Tension band wiring."],["What is a bumper fracture?","Fracture of the lateral tibial plateau from a valgus force, like a car bumper hitting the knee."],["Test for meniscus tear?","McMurray test and Apley grind test, plus joint line tenderness."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Fractures of the lower limb"},{b:"magee",ch:"Knee"},{c:"Kise NJ, et al. Exercise therapy versus arthroscopic partial meniscectomy for degenerative meniscal tear in middle aged patients. BMJ. 2016;354:i3740.", q:"Kise exercise therapy versus arthroscopic partial meniscectomy BMJ 2016"}],
  keywords:"patella fracture tibial plateau tibia shaft meniscus collateral ligament knee injuries",
  mcq:[["Displaced transverse fracture of patella is best treated by:",["Cylinder cast","Tension band wiring","Patellectomy always","Traction"],1,"Tension band wiring converts tensile forces into compression."],["Unhappy triad includes all EXCEPT:",["ACL","MCL","Medial meniscus","PCL"],3,"O'Donoghue's triad is ACL, MCL and medial meniscus."]]
},
{
  id:"co-recurrent-patellar-dislocation", title:"Recurrent dislocation of shoulder and patella", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Knee and shoulder", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["recurrent dislocation of patella","patellar instability","recurrent shoulder dislocation","MPFL reconstruction","Bankart lesion","habitual dislocation"],
  simple:"Some joints, once dislocated, keep coming out again. The kneecap usually slips outwards in young girls with loose ligaments or a shallow groove. The shoulder usually slips forwards after a first injury that tears the front of the socket. Strengthening the right muscles helps, and surgery tightens or rebuilds the torn structures if it keeps happening.",
  flow:["First traumatic dislocation, laxity or bony malalignment","Torn stabilisers: MPFL in knee, Bankart lesion in shoulder","Repeated giving way, apprehension, episodes with minor force","Apprehension tests, X-ray, MRI, measure Q angle or bone loss","Stabilising exercise; surgery if recurrent","Phased post-op rehab and return to activity"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Recurrent dislocation of patella: causes", p:["Almost always lateral dislocation, common in adolescent girls.","Predisposing factors: generalised laxity, patella alta, trochlear dysplasia (shallow groove), increased Q angle and lateral tibial tuberosity, genu valgum, femoral anteversion, weak VMO, tight lateral retinaculum.","Medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) tear is the essential lesion after the first dislocation."]},
    {h:"Patella: features and investigations", p:["Knee gives way on twisting, swelling, apprehension (Fairbank) test positive when patella is pushed laterally.","Increased lateral glide, J-sign on extension.","X-ray: skyline view, lateral view for patella alta (Insall-Salvati ratio); MRI for MPFL and osteochondral fracture; CT for TT-TG distance."]},
    {h:"Patella: management", p:["Conservative: knee brace with lateral buttress, VMO and hip abductor and external rotator strengthening, closed chain exercise, taping (McConnell), proprioception.","Surgery: MPFL reconstruction; tibial tubercle transfer (Fulkerson or Elmslie-Trillat) for malalignment; trochleoplasty for severe dysplasia; lateral release alone is no longer recommended.","Post-op physio: brace locked in extension early, SLR, progressive flexion (0-90 degrees by about 6 weeks), return to sport around 4-6 months."]},
    {h:"Recurrent dislocation of shoulder (summary)", p:["Usually anterior after a traumatic first dislocation in a young person; Bankart lesion (anterior-inferior labrum) and Hill-Sachs lesion (humeral head defect).","TUBS versus AMBRI types; apprehension and relocation tests.","Surgery: arthroscopic Bankart repair or Latarjet procedure for bone loss; older Putti-Platt and Bristow operations.","Physio: rotator cuff and scapular stabiliser strengthening, proprioception, avoid abduction-external rotation early after repair."]},
    {h:"Comparison", p:["Both: young patients, first traumatic episode common, essential soft tissue lesion (MPFL vs Bankart), dynamic muscle stabilisers (VMO vs rotator cuff), surgery for repeated episodes.","Patella: bony malalignment matters more; shoulder: glenoid and humeral bone loss matters more."]},
    {h:"Complications and conclusion", p:["Osteochondral fractures, patellofemoral arthritis, stiffness, recurrence after surgery.","Treatment targets the underlying cause: muscle balance for laxity, ligament reconstruction for MPFL or labral tears, bony realignment for malalignment."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a skyline view of the knee showing the patella sitting in the trochlear groove, with an arrow showing lateral escape and the MPFL as a line on the medial side. Next to it, draw a glenoid with a Bankart tear marked anteroinferiorly.",
  evidence:[["MPFL reconstruction for recurrent patellar instability","Low redislocation rates","Moderate"],["Conservative rehab after first-time patellar dislocation","Reasonable outcomes; surgery if recurrent or osteochondral fracture","Moderate"],["Arthroscopic stabilisation in young first-time shoulder dislocators","Lower recurrence than sling and rehab alone","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Systematic reviews on patellar instability and shoulder instability surgery",
  outcomes:[["Kujala score","Patellofemoral function"],["Apprehension tests","Instability"],["WOSI","Shoulder instability quality of life"],["Single leg hop","Functional readiness"]],
  recent:["MPFL reconstruction has replaced isolated lateral release as the main soft tissue procedure for recurrent patellar dislocation."],
  mnemonic:{word:"MB", items:[["M","MPFL: patella's key lesion"],["B","Bankart: shoulder's key lesion"]], extra:"Patella goes lateral; shoulder goes anterior."},
  example:"A 15-year-old girl in Coimbatore has had three episodes of her kneecap slipping out while dancing. She has patella alta and weak VMO; after a failed 3-month programme she undergoes MPFL reconstruction followed by phased rehab.",
  flags:["Large haemarthrosis after dislocation (possible osteochondral fracture)","Locked knee after patellar dislocation (loose body)","Numbness over deltoid after shoulder dislocation","Recurrent episodes with minimal force despite rehab"],
  viva:[["Direction of patellar dislocation?","Almost always lateral."],["Name the key soft tissue restraint against lateral patellar displacement.","Medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL)."],["What is Hill-Sachs lesion?","An impression defect on the posterolateral humeral head after anterior shoulder dislocation."]],
  refs:[{b:"brukner",ch:"Anterior knee pain and patellar instability"},{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Recurrent dislocations"},{b:"magee",ch:"Knee and shoulder"}],
  keywords:"recurrent dislocation patella shoulder MPFL Bankart Hill-Sachs instability",
  mcq:[["The essential lesion in recurrent patellar dislocation is tear of the:",["ACL","MPFL","Lateral meniscus","Patellar tendon"],1,"MPFL is the main medial restraint and tears in lateral dislocation."],["A positive Fairbank apprehension test indicates:",["Meniscus tear","Patellar instability","ACL tear","Patellar tendinopathy"],1,"Apprehension when the patella is pushed laterally indicates instability."]]
},
{
  id:"co-amputation-principles", title:"Amputations: classification, indications, principles of surgery and complications", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Limbs", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["amputation classification","indications for amputation","levels of amputation","stump complications","guillotine amputation","Syme amputation"],
  simple:"Amputation is the surgical removal of part or all of a limb. It is done when the limb is dead, dangerous to life, or useless and painful. Surgeons choose a level that heals well and suits a prosthesis, and the stump must be shaped and strengthened for walking with an artificial limb.",
  flow:["Dead, deadly or dead-loss limb (gangrene, tumour, trauma)","Choose level for healing and prosthetic fit","Surgery: skin flaps, muscle myodesis, nerve and bone handling","Post-op: stump care, positioning, bandaging","Pre-prosthetic and prosthetic training","Prevent and manage complications"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definition and classification", p:["Amputation: removal of a limb or part through bone; disarticulation: through a joint.","By urgency: emergency (guillotine or open, for infection), elective (closed with flaps).","By level (lower limb): toe, ray, transmetatarsal, Lisfranc, Chopart, Syme (ankle disarticulation), transtibial (below knee), knee disarticulation, transfemoral (above knee), hip disarticulation, hemipelvectomy.","Upper limb: digit, transcarpal, wrist disarticulation, transradial, elbow disarticulation, transhumeral, shoulder disarticulation, forequarter.","Congenital limb deficiency is classified separately (ISO terminology)."]},
    {h:"Indications (3 Ds)", p:["Dead: gangrene from peripheral vascular disease, diabetes, burns, frostbite, crush injury.","Deadly: malignant tumour, gas gangrene, uncontrolled spreading infection.","Dead loss (dead nuisance): severe deformity, painful useless limb, failed reconstruction, irreparable nerve or vascular injury."]},
    {h:"Principles of surgery", p:["Level: as distal as possible for function, but proximal enough to heal; preserve the knee whenever possible (energy cost rises greatly above knee).","Ideal transtibial stump about 12-18 cm (roughly 15 cm) from the joint line; transfemoral about 25 cm from tip of greater trochanter.","Skin: long posterior flap in transtibial; scar away from weight-bearing area.","Muscle: myodesis (muscle sutured to bone) or myoplasty for stability and good shape.","Nerves: pulled down and cut cleanly to retract (reduce neuroma); bone: bevelled edges, fibula cut slightly shorter than tibia; haemostasis and drain."]},
    {h:"Complications", p:["Early: haemorrhage, haematoma, infection, wound breakdown and flap necrosis, DVT.","Late: stump oedema, joint contracture (hip flexion-abduction, knee flexion), painful neuroma, phantom limb sensation and phantom pain, bony spur, adherent scar, skin problems from socket, stump ulcer, osteoporosis."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy management (summary)", p:["Pre-op: chest care, strengthening of upper limbs and sound limb, crutch walking, counselling.","Post-op: positioning (prone lying 20-30 min 2-3 times daily, no pillow under stump), stump bandaging in figure-of-eight or shrinker, desensitisation, strengthening hip extensors and abductors, knee extensors.","Early walking aid (PPAM aid), then prosthetic training; see lower limb amputation rehab topic."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Good surgical technique and early stump care decide whether the patient walks well with a prosthesis."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a full lower limb outline with horizontal lines at each amputation level and label them from toe to hemipelvectomy. Mark the ideal transtibial stump length.",
  evidence:[["Rigid removable dressings after transtibial amputation","Faster readiness for prosthesis than soft dressings","Low-moderate"],["Preserving the knee joint","Lower energy cost and better walking","Moderate"],["Mirror therapy for phantom limb pain","Some reduction in pain","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"Amputation surgery and rehabilitation texts; systematic reviews of post-operative dressings",
  outcomes:[["Stump girth","Oedema and shaping"],["Goniometry of hip and knee","Contracture"],["AMPPRO","Prosthetic mobility"],["Phantom pain VAS","Pain"]],
  recent:["Targeted muscle reinnervation and osseointegration are newer surgical options in specialised centres for neuroma pain and socket problems."],
  mnemonic:{word:"3D", items:[["D","Dead"],["D","Deadly"],["D","Dead loss"]], extra:"Save the knee whenever possible."},
  example:"A 58-year-old diabetic in Salem has gangrene of the forefoot spreading to the leg and undergoes transtibial amputation with a long posterior flap. The physio teaches prone lying, figure-of-eight bandaging and hip extensor work.",
  flags:["Wound discoloration or discharge (flap necrosis or infection)","Knee flexion contracture developing (avoid pillow under knee)","Severe burning phantom pain affecting sleep","Ulcer on the other diabetic foot"],
  viva:[["What is Syme amputation?","Ankle disarticulation with heel pad preserved for end-bearing."],["Why cut the fibula shorter than the tibia?","To avoid a painful prominent fibular end and give a better conical stump."],["Common contracture after transfemoral amputation?","Hip flexion and abduction."]],
  refs:[{b:"osullivan",ch:"Amputation"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Amputations."},{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Amputations"}],
  keywords:"amputation classification indications levels principles complications stump phantom",
  mcq:[["Indication 'deadly' for amputation includes:",["Painful useless limb","Gas gangrene","Congenital deformity","Frostbite of toe only"],1,"Gas gangrene is life-threatening (deadly)."],["Commonly recommended transtibial stump length is about:",["5 cm","15 cm","30 cm","45 cm"],1,"Around 12-18 cm below the knee joint line is usually ideal."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-bone-joint-infections", title:"Bone and joint infections: septic arthritis, osteomyelitis and tuberculosis of bones and joints including spinal TB", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"General", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["acute osteomyelitis","chronic osteomyelitis","septic arthritis","pyogenic arthritis","tuberculosis of hip","Pott's spine","skeletal tuberculosis"],
  simple:"Bacteria can reach bone or a joint through the blood or through a wound. In bone they cause osteomyelitis; in a joint they cause septic arthritis, which can destroy cartilage within days. Tuberculosis is a slower infection that eats away joints and the spine. Early antibiotics, surgical drainage and careful positioning save the joint; physiotherapy prevents stiffness and deformity.",
  flow:["Blood-borne or direct entry of Staph aureus or TB bacilli","Pus under pressure destroys bone or cartilage","Fever, severe pain, swelling, pseudoparalysis or cold abscess","Blood counts, CRP, aspiration, X-ray, MRI","Antibiotics or ATT, drainage, splint in functional position","Graded ROM, strengthening, mobility once infection settles"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Acute osteomyelitis", p:["Infection of bone, commonest in children at the metaphysis of long bones (lower femur, upper tibia) because of slow, looping metaphyseal blood flow.","Organism: Staphylococcus aureus most often; Salmonella in sickle cell disease.","Features: high fever, severe local pain, refusal to move limb, metaphyseal tenderness; X-ray normal for the first 10-14 days, then periosteal reaction.","Investigations: raised WBC, ESR, CRP, blood culture, MRI early; aspiration of subperiosteal pus.","Treatment: IV then oral antibiotics for about 4-6 weeks, splintage, surgical drainage if pus or no response in 48 hours."]},
    {h:"Chronic osteomyelitis", p:["Follows inadequately treated acute infection, open fracture or surgery.","Sequestrum (dead bone), involucrum (new bone around it), cloaca and discharging sinus.","Treatment: sequestrectomy, saucerisation, debridement, antibiotic beads, bone grafting; Ilizarov for gaps.","Complications: pathological fracture, growth disturbance, amyloidosis, squamous cell carcinoma in a long-standing sinus (Marjolin ulcer)."]},
    {h:"Septic arthritis", p:["Pyogenic infection of a joint; commonest in infants (hip) and in adults with diabetes, RA, steroid use or after injection.","Proteolytic enzymes destroy articular cartilage quickly, so it is an orthopaedic emergency.","Features: acutely painful, hot, swollen joint held in position of maximum capsular volume (hip in flexion, abduction, external rotation), all movements painful.","Diagnosis: joint aspiration (turbid fluid, high WBC, Gram stain, culture), ultrasound for hip effusion, raised CRP; Kocher criteria in children.","Treatment: urgent arthrotomy or arthroscopic washout plus IV antibiotics; complications include dislocation, ankylosis, growth arrest and Tom Smith arthritis in infants."]},
    {h:"Tuberculosis of bones and joints", p:["Always secondary to a primary focus (lung, lymph nodes); spine is commonest (about half), then hip and knee.","Pathology: caseating granuloma, cold abscess, slow destruction, osteopenia, little new bone formation.","Hip TB stages: synovitis (flexion, abduction, external rotation, apparent lengthening), early arthritis, advanced arthritis (flexion, adduction, internal rotation, true shortening).","Spinal TB (Pott's spine): paradiscal type commonest; disc space narrowing, vertebral collapse, gibbus, cold abscess (psoas), Pott's paraplegia.","Treatment: multidrug anti-tubercular therapy for 9-18 months (per national programme), rest in functional position, surgery for abscess, neurological deficit, instability or failure (anterior decompression and fusion)."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy", p:["Acute phase: splint in functional position (knee in slight flexion, hip in neutral with traction), static contractions, chest care, positioning to avoid deformity.","After infection is controlled (pain and CRP falling): gentle active ROM, progressive strengthening, gait training with partial weight bearing.","Spinal TB: bed rest or brace initially, breathing exercises, isometric back and limb exercises; in Pott's paraplegia, pressure care and neurological rehab; spinal brace (Taylor or anterior spinal hyperextension) for mobilisation.","If ankylosis is expected, aim for fusion in the most functional position."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Early diagnosis, aspiration and appropriate antibiotics prevent permanent damage; physiotherapy maintains function without spreading infection, starting active movement only when infection is controlled."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a long bone with metaphyseal abscess lifting the periosteum, then label sequestrum, involucrum and cloaca with a sinus to skin. For spinal TB draw two vertebrae collapsing anteriorly around a destroyed disc forming a gibbus, with a psoas abscess tracking down.",
  evidence:[["Shorter IV to early oral antibiotic switch in childhood osteomyelitis","Similar outcomes to prolonged IV therapy","Moderate"],["Surgical washout plus antibiotics for septic arthritis","Standard care; delay worsens joint outcome","Moderate"],["Multidrug ATT for spinal TB","Healing in most patients; surgery for deficit or deformity","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Orthopaedic texts and systematic reviews on paediatric bone and joint infection and spinal tuberculosis",
  outcomes:[["CRP and ESR trend","Infection activity"],["Joint ROM and pain","Joint function"],["Frankel / ASIA grade","Neurology in Pott's paraplegia"],["Gait and ADL","Functional recovery"]],
  recent:["MRI and early oral antibiotic switch have shortened hospital stay in childhood osteomyelitis.","Gene-based tests such as GeneXpert help rapid diagnosis of skeletal TB and drug resistance."],
  mnemonic:{word:"SIC", items:[["S","Sequestrum: dead bone"],["I","Involucrum: new bone shell"],["C","Cloaca: hole for pus"]], extra:"Septic joint = emergency washout."},
  example:"A 6-year-old boy in Madurai has fever and refuses to walk for 2 days, with a hip held flexed and abducted. Ultrasound shows effusion and aspiration gives pus; after urgent washout and antibiotics, physio starts gentle ROM and later gait training.",
  flags:["Infant with irritable hip and fever (septic arthritis, emergency)","Back pain with fever, weight loss and new leg weakness (spinal TB with cord compression)","Immunocompromised patient with joint swelling","Never apply heat or vigorous mobilisation to an actively infected joint"],
  viva:[["Commonest site of acute haematogenous osteomyelitis in children?","Metaphysis of long bones, especially lower femur and upper tibia."],["What is a sequestrum?","A piece of dead bone separated from living bone in chronic osteomyelitis."],["Commonest type of spinal TB?","Paradiscal type."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Infections of bones and joints"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapters: Infections of bones and joints; Tuberculosis of bones and joints."},{b:"magee",ch:"Hip and spine examination"}],
  keywords:"osteomyelitis septic arthritis tuberculosis Pott's spine sequestrum involucrum cold abscess",
  mcq:[["The commonest organism causing acute osteomyelitis is:",["Streptococcus pyogenes","Staphylococcus aureus","E. coli","Mycobacterium tuberculosis"],1,"Staph aureus causes most cases at all ages."],["In early tuberculosis of the hip the attitude is:",["Flexion, adduction, internal rotation","Extension, adduction, internal rotation","Flexion, abduction, external rotation","Extension, abduction, internal rotation"],2,"The synovitis stage gives flexion, abduction and external rotation with apparent lengthening."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-bone-tumours", title:"Bone tumours: osteoma, osteosarcoma, osteoclastoma (giant cell tumour), Ewing sarcoma and multiple myeloma", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"General", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["bone tumours","osteosarcoma","giant cell tumour","osteoclastoma","Ewing sarcoma","multiple myeloma","osteoid osteoma"],
  simple:"Bone tumours can be benign (like an osteoma) or malignant (like osteosarcoma and Ewing sarcoma). Age and site help guess the tumour: teenagers near the knee suggest osteosarcoma, young adults at the bone end suggest giant cell tumour, older adults with many bone holes suggest myeloma. Treatment ranges from simple removal to chemotherapy, limb salvage surgery or amputation, and physiotherapy restores function safely.",
  flow:["Abnormal growth of bone or marrow cells","Bone destruction or new tumour bone, weakened bone","Pain worse at night, swelling, pathological fracture","X-ray pattern, MRI, CT chest, bone scan, biopsy","Excision, chemo or radiotherapy, limb salvage or amputation","Protected mobility, strengthening, prosthetic or endoprosthetic rehab"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Classification", p:["Benign bone forming: osteoma, osteoid osteoma; malignant bone forming: osteosarcoma.","Cartilage: osteochondroma, enchondroma (benign); chondrosarcoma (malignant).","Others: giant cell tumour (locally aggressive), Ewing sarcoma (small round cell), multiple myeloma (plasma cell, marrow).","Secondary (metastatic) deposits from breast, prostate, lung, kidney and thyroid are the commonest malignant bone lesions in adults."]},
    {h:"Benign: osteoma and osteoid osteoma", p:["Osteoma: dense ivory bone growth, often skull or facial sinuses; usually painless; excised only if symptomatic.","Osteoid osteoma: young person, small nidus under 1.5 cm with surrounding sclerosis, night pain dramatically relieved by aspirin or NSAIDs; treated by radiofrequency ablation or excision."]},
    {h:"Osteosarcoma", p:["Commonest primary malignant bone tumour in adolescents (10-25 years); metaphysis around the knee, upper humerus.","X-ray: mixed destruction and new bone, Codman triangle, sunray spicules.","Spreads early to lungs; CT chest for staging, biopsy for diagnosis.","Treatment: neoadjuvant chemotherapy, wide excision with limb salvage (endoprosthesis) or amputation, then adjuvant chemotherapy."]},
    {h:"Giant cell tumour and Ewing sarcoma", p:["Giant cell tumour (osteoclastoma): age 20-40 after physeal closure, epiphysis-metaphysis of lower femur, upper tibia, lower radius; eccentric lytic soap-bubble lesion; locally aggressive and recurs; extended curettage with bone cement or graft, or excision.","Ewing sarcoma: age 5-20, diaphysis of long bones or pelvis, fever and raised ESR mimicking infection, onion-peel periosteal reaction; small round blue cells; chemotherapy plus surgery and/or radiotherapy (radiosensitive)."]},
    {h:"Multiple myeloma", p:["Malignant plasma cell proliferation in older adults (over 50).","Bone pain (often back), punched-out lytic lesions in skull, spine, pelvis; vertebral collapse, hypercalcaemia, anaemia, renal failure, infections.","Investigations: serum protein electrophoresis (M band), Bence Jones proteinuria, bone marrow plasma cells.","Treatment: chemotherapy, targeted drugs, stem cell transplant, bisphosphonates, radiotherapy and fixation for pathological fractures."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy", p:["Avoid deep massage, heat, ultrasound and electrotherapy over a tumour site.","Know weight-bearing status: lytic lesions risk pathological fracture, so avoid resisted rotation and heavy loading of affected bone; no vigorous spinal mobilisation in myeloma.","Post limb salvage: protected ROM per surgeon, isometrics, progressive strengthening, gait training; after amputation, stump care and prosthetic training.","Exercise during chemotherapy reduces fatigue; monitor blood counts (low platelets or Hb) before exercise.","Palliative care: pain relief, positioning, mobility aids, energy conservation."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Age, site and X-ray appearance guide diagnosis, but biopsy confirms it. Physiotherapy must respect fracture risk and haematology values while maximising function."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a long bone divided into epiphysis, metaphysis and diaphysis and write the typical tumour in each: GCT in epiphysis, osteosarcoma in metaphysis, Ewing in diaphysis. Add a small sketch of the Codman triangle and sunray appearance.",
  evidence:[["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy with limb salvage surgery for osteosarcoma","Survival similar to amputation in suitable patients","Moderate"],["Exercise during cancer treatment","Reduces fatigue, improves function","High"],["Bisphosphonates in myeloma","Fewer skeletal events","High"]],
  evidenceSource:"Orthopaedic oncology texts and systematic reviews on exercise in cancer",
  outcomes:[["MSTS score","Function after limb salvage"],["Pain scale","Symptom control"],["6-minute walk","Endurance"],["ECOG performance status","Overall function"]],
  recent:["Modular endoprostheses and expandable implants for children have made limb salvage the usual choice in osteosarcoma.","Newer myeloma drugs and stem cell transplant have improved survival, increasing the need for rehabilitation."],
  mnemonic:{word:"GEM", items:[["G","GCT: Epiphysis, 20-40 years"],["E","Ewing: Diaphysis, onion peel"],["M","Metaphysis: osteosarcoma, sunray"]], extra:"Myeloma: older adult, punched-out lesions."},
  example:"A 14-year-old boy in Chennai has knee pain for 2 months, worse at night, with a hard swelling above the knee. X-ray shows sunray spicules; after biopsy and chemotherapy he undergoes limb salvage with an endoprosthesis and begins protected rehab.",
  flags:["Night pain unrelated to activity","Rapidly enlarging hard swelling","Pathological fracture with minor trauma","Back pain with anaemia and raised ESR in older adult (myeloma)"],
  viva:[["What is Codman triangle?","Periosteum lifted by tumour forming a triangle of new bone at the edge, seen in osteosarcoma."],["Typical X-ray of giant cell tumour?","Eccentric, expansile lytic soap-bubble lesion at the bone end."],["Onion-peel appearance is seen in?","Ewing sarcoma."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Bone tumours"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Bone tumours."},{b:"goodman",ch:"Oncology and bone tumours"}],
  keywords:"bone tumour osteosarcoma giant cell tumour osteoclastoma Ewing sarcoma multiple myeloma osteoma Codman",
  mcq:[["Soap-bubble appearance at the lower end of femur in a 30-year-old suggests:",["Osteosarcoma","Giant cell tumour","Ewing sarcoma","Osteoid osteoma"],1,"GCT is an eccentric expansile epiphyseal lesion after physeal closure."],["Bence Jones proteins in urine are typical of:",["Osteosarcoma","Ewing sarcoma","Multiple myeloma","Osteoma"],2,"Light chains from malignant plasma cells appear in urine."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-arthritis-deformities", title:"Rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis and ankylosing spondylitis: pathology and deformities", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"General", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["RA deformities","swan neck deformity","boutonniere deformity","osteoarthritis pathology","ankylosing spondylitis deformity","bamboo spine","OA knee varus"],
  simple:"Rheumatoid arthritis starts in the joint lining and slowly destroys cartilage and ligaments, so joints drift into typical deformities. Osteoarthritis is wear and failed repair of cartilage with bony spurs. Ankylosing spondylitis inflames where ligaments attach to the spine, so the spine stiffens and bends forward. Knowing the pathology tells you which deformity to prevent.",
  flow:["Autoimmune synovitis (RA), cartilage failure (OA), enthesitis (AS)","Pannus, erosions; osteophytes; syndesmophytes and fusion","Characteristic deformities in hands, knees, spine","Clinical exam, X-ray, ESR/CRP, RF, anti-CCP, HLA-B27","Drugs, joint protection, splints, surgery","Exercise, posture and function training lifelong"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Rheumatoid arthritis: pathology", p:["Chronic autoimmune symmetrical polyarthritis of small joints, women 3 times more than men.","Synovitis forms a pannus (inflamed granulation tissue) that erodes cartilage and bone at joint margins; capsule and ligaments stretch, tendons rupture.","Stages: synovitis, destruction, deformity (fixed), and bony or fibrous ankylosis.","Extra-articular: nodules, anaemia, lung fibrosis, vasculitis, atlantoaxial instability."]},
    {h:"RA deformities", p:["Hand: ulnar deviation and volar subluxation at MCP, swan neck (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion), boutonniere (PIP flexion, DIP hyperextension), Z-thumb, radial deviation of wrist, extensor tendon rupture.","Elbow flexion deformity; shoulder stiffness.","Knee: flexion and valgus; hip: protrusio acetabuli; foot: hallux valgus, clawed toes, MTP subluxation, flat foot.","Cervical spine: atlantoaxial subluxation (danger with neck manipulation or forceful flexion)."]},
    {h:"Osteoarthritis: pathology and deformities", p:["Degenerative failure of articular cartilage with attempted repair: fibrillation, loss of joint space (often asymmetric), subchondral sclerosis, cysts and osteophytes.","Primary (age, obesity, genetics) or secondary (trauma, malalignment, infection, Perthes, DDH).","Deformities: knee varus (medial compartment), flexion deformity; hip flexion, adduction, external rotation with shortening; Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes; hallux valgus or rigidus.","X-ray: narrowed joint space, osteophytes, sclerosis, cysts (Kellgren-Lawrence grading)."]},
    {h:"Ankylosing spondylitis: pathology and deformities", p:["Seronegative inflammatory spondyloarthritis of young men, strongly associated with HLA-B27.","Enthesitis at ligament and capsule insertions, starting at the sacroiliac joints, then ossification forms syndesmophytes, squaring of vertebrae and bamboo spine.","Deformities: loss of lumbar lordosis, thoracic kyphosis, forward-poked neck (question-mark posture), hip flexion contractures, reduced chest expansion.","Fusion makes the spine brittle: even minor falls can cause unstable spinal fracture."]},
    {h:"Orthopaedic management", p:["RA: DMARDs (methotrexate), biologics, steroids; synovectomy, tendon repair or transfer, arthrodesis of wrist or thumb, MCP arthroplasty, TKR or THR.","OA: weight loss, analgesics, intra-articular injection; high tibial osteotomy for medial knee OA in younger patient; unicompartmental or total joint replacement.","AS: NSAIDs, anti-TNF biologics; THR for hip ankylosis; spinal osteotomy for severe fixed kyphosis."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["RA: rest and resting splints in acute flare, gentle ROM, isometrics; in remission progressive strengthening, joint protection and energy conservation, hydrotherapy, paraffin wax for hands.","OA: education, weight management, quadriceps and hip strengthening, aerobic exercise, aids (cane in opposite hand).","AS: daily extension exercises, deep breathing and chest expansion, prone lying, posture training, swimming; avoid spinal manipulation."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Pathology predicts deformity: pannus in RA, cartilage loss in OA, enthesitis and fusion in AS. Early drug control plus lifelong exercise prevents most fixed deformities."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a three-column table with RA, OA and AS rows (pathology, joints, X-ray, deformity). Add small finger sketches for swan neck and boutonniere and a stick figure in question-mark posture for AS.",
  evidence:[["Exercise therapy in knee and hip OA","Reduces pain and improves function","High"],["Supervised exercise in AS","Improves mobility and function","Moderate"],["Exercise and joint protection in RA","Improves strength and function without increasing disease activity","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane and systematic reviews on exercise in OA, RA and AS",
  outcomes:[["DAS28","RA disease activity"],["WOMAC","OA pain and function"],["BASFI and BASMI","AS function and spinal mobility"],["Grip strength","Hand function"]],
  recent:["Treat-to-target with early DMARDs and biologics has reduced the number of RA patients reaching fixed deformities."],
  mnemonic:{word:"PEP", items:[["P","Pannus: RA erodes"],["E","Enthesitis: AS fuses"],["P","Partial cartilage loss with spurs: OA"]], extra:"Swan neck: PIP up; Boutonniere: PIP down."},
  example:"A 45-year-old woman in Salem has morning stiffness lasting 2 hours and ulnar drift at both MCP joints. She is started on methotrexate, given resting splints and taught joint protection and gentle hand exercises.",
  flags:["Neck pain with tingling in hands in RA (atlantoaxial instability)","Minor fall in AS with new neck or back pain (possible fracture)","Hot single swollen joint in RA (exclude septic arthritis)","Avoid vigorous stretching of inflamed RA joints"],
  viva:[["What is pannus?","Inflamed proliferating synovial granulation tissue that erodes cartilage and bone in RA."],["Name nodes seen in OA of fingers.","Heberden nodes at DIP and Bouchard nodes at PIP."],["Which gene is associated with AS?","HLA-B27."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Arthritis"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapters: Rheumatoid arthritis; Osteoarthritis; Ankylosing spondylitis."},{b:"magee",ch:"Hand, knee and spine"}],
  keywords:"rheumatoid arthritis osteoarthritis ankylosing spondylitis pathology deformities swan neck boutonniere bamboo spine",
  mcq:[["Swan neck deformity consists of:",["PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension","PIP hyperextension and DIP flexion","MCP extension and IP flexion","Thumb Z deformity"],1,"PIP hyperextends and DIP flexes in swan neck."],["Medial compartment knee OA usually produces:",["Genu valgum","Genu recurvatum","Genu varum","Patella alta"],2,"Loss of medial joint space tilts the knee into varus."]]
}
