{
  id:"co-spinal-deformities", title:"Spinal deformities: classification and management of scoliosis, kyphosis and lordosis", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Spine", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["scoliosis classification","kyphosis","Scheuermann disease","hyperlordosis","structural scoliosis","postural scoliosis","Cobb angle"],
  simple:"The spine normally has gentle front-to-back curves and is straight when seen from behind. Scoliosis is a sideways curve with twisting, kyphosis is too much forward rounding, and lordosis is too much backward hollowing. Some curves are just bad posture and correct on bending; others are fixed. Treatment depends on cause, size of curve and growth left.",
  flow:["Idiopathic, congenital, neuromuscular, degenerative or postural cause","Abnormal curve: lateral with rotation, or excessive sagittal curve","Rib hump, shoulder asymmetry, round back, hollow back","Adams test, Cobb angle on X-ray, Risser sign, neuro exam","Observe, exercise, brace or fuse depending on curve","Posture, curve-specific exercise, post-op rehab"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definitions", p:["Scoliosis: lateral curvature of the spine of 10 degrees or more on X-ray with vertebral rotation.","Kyphosis: excessive posterior convexity, usually thoracic (normal about 20-45 degrees).","Lordosis: excessive anterior convexity, usually lumbar or cervical.","Gibbus: sharp angular kyphosis from collapse of one or two vertebrae (TB, fracture)."]},
    {h:"Classification", p:["Scoliosis: non-structural (postural, compensatory to leg length discrepancy, sciatic, hysterical) which corrects on forward bending or lying; structural with fixed rotation.","Structural types: idiopathic (infantile, juvenile, adolescent; adolescent commonest, girls, right thoracic), congenital (hemivertebra, failure of segmentation), neuromuscular (CP, polio, DMD, SMA), syndromes (Marfan, neurofibromatosis), degenerative.","Kyphosis: postural, Scheuermann (wedging of 3 or more vertebrae of 5 degrees or more), congenital, TB, osteoporotic, AS, traumatic.","Lordosis: postural, secondary to hip flexion contracture, spondylolisthesis, obesity, pregnancy, muscular dystrophy."]},
    {h:"Clinical features and investigations", p:["Shoulder and waist asymmetry, scapular prominence, rib hump on Adams forward bend test, scoliometer reading.","Kyphosis: round back, compensatory lordosis, tight hamstrings and pectorals; Scheuermann curve does not correct on extension.","Examine neurology, leg length, skin (cafe-au-lait, hairy patch).","Standing full spine X-ray: Cobb angle, Risser sign for skeletal maturity; MRI if atypical or neurological signs."]},
    {h:"Orthopaedic management", p:["Idiopathic scoliosis: Cobb under 25 degrees observe with 4-6 monthly X-ray; 25-45 degrees in a growing child brace (Boston TLSO, Milwaukee for high curves) about 18-23 hours a day; over 45-50 degrees spinal fusion with pedicle screws and rods.","Congenital: early fusion or hemivertebra excision if progressive; growing rods in young children.","Neuromuscular: seating, bracing for sitting balance, fusion for progressive curves.","Scheuermann: exercise and bracing in growing child, surgery for curves over about 70-75 degrees or pain."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["Scoliosis-specific exercise (Schroth, SEAS): autocorrection, rotational breathing, elongation; supports bracing but is not a substitute for brace in progressive curves.","Brace care: skin checks, exercises in and out of brace, breathing exercises.","Kyphosis and lordosis: posture education, pectoral and hip flexor stretching, back extensor and abdominal strengthening, core stability.","Post-fusion: early mobilisation, log rolling, breathing exercise, walking; avoid bending, lifting and twisting for about 3-6 months."]},
    {h:"Complications and conclusion", p:["Severe thoracic curves (over about 70-80 degrees) reduce lung capacity; progression continues in adults if over 50 degrees.","Classify first (structural or not), measure Cobb angle and maturity, then choose observe, brace or fuse."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a posterior view of the spine with a right thoracic curve, label the apex and draw two lines along the end plates of the most tilted vertebrae to show how the Cobb angle is measured. Add a side view showing kyphosis and lordosis.",
  evidence:[["Bracing in adolescent idiopathic scoliosis (BrAIST trial)","Reduced progression to surgical threshold","High"],["Scoliosis-specific exercise","May reduce progression in mild curves","Low to moderate"],["Spinal fusion for large curves","Corrects and stabilises curve","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"BrAIST trial (Weinstein et al, NEJM 2013) and SOSORT guidelines",
  outcomes:[["Cobb angle","Curve size"],["Scoliometer ATR","Rotation"],["SRS-22","Quality of life"],["Risser sign","Growth remaining"]],
  recent:["Vertebral body tethering is a newer growth-modulating option for selected adolescent curves; long-term results are still being studied."],
  mnemonic:{word:"OBS", items:[["O","Observe under 25 degrees"],["B","Brace 25-45 degrees if growing"],["S","Surgery over 45-50 degrees"]], extra:"Postural curve corrects on bending; structural does not."},
  example:"A 13-year-old girl in Trichy is noticed by her tailor to have one shoulder higher. Adams test shows a right rib hump and Cobb angle is 28 degrees with Risser 1; she is given a Boston brace and Schroth exercises.",
  flags:["Painful scoliosis (exclude tumour such as osteoid osteoma or infection)","Left thoracic curve or neurological signs (MRI needed)","Rapid progression during growth spurt","Breathlessness with severe thoracic curve"],
  viva:[["What is Cobb angle?","Angle between lines drawn along the end plates of the most tilted vertebrae at the top and bottom of a curve."],["How to differentiate postural from structural scoliosis?","Postural scoliosis disappears on forward bending; structural shows a rib hump."],["Definition of Scheuermann disease?","Anterior wedging of 5 degrees or more in 3 or more adjacent vertebrae causing rigid kyphosis."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Spinal deformities"},{c:"Weinstein SL et al. Effects of bracing in adolescents with idiopathic scoliosis. N Engl J Med 2013.", q:"Weinstein bracing adolescent idiopathic scoliosis 2013"},{b:"kisner",ch:"The spine: posture"}],
  keywords:"scoliosis kyphosis lordosis spinal deformity Cobb angle Scheuermann brace fusion",
  mcq:[["A structural scoliosis is identified by:",["Curve disappears on lying","Rib hump on forward bending","Leg length discrepancy","Correction on side bending only"],1,"Fixed rotation produces a rib hump in the Adams test."],["Bracing in adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is usually advised for a growing child with Cobb angle of:",["5-10 degrees","10-20 degrees","25-45 degrees","Over 60 degrees"],2,"Brace range is roughly 25-45 degrees with growth remaining."]]
}
