{
  id:"co-poliomyelitis", title:"Poliomyelitis: pathology, prevention, residual paralysis and muscle (tendon) transfers", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Lower limb", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["polio","acute flaccid paralysis","residual polio paralysis","post-polio deformities","tendon transfer in polio","pulse polio","anterior horn cell"],
  simple:"Polio is a virus that destroys the motor nerve cells in the front of the spinal cord, causing floppy, uneven paralysis with normal sensation. India is now polio-free thanks to vaccination, but many adults live with the leftover weakness and deformities. Treatment uses exercises, calipers, release of tight tissues and moving a working tendon to replace a paralysed muscle.",
  flow:["Poliovirus enters by faeco-oral route","Destroys anterior horn cells: asymmetric flaccid paralysis","Fever, muscle pain, then paralysis; sensation normal","Muscle charting, deformity and leg length assessment","Splinting, calipers, soft tissue release, tendon transfer, osteotomy","Re-education of transfer, gait training, orthotic use"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Pathology", p:["Enterovirus spread by faeco-oral route; multiplies in gut, enters blood and attacks anterior horn cells of spinal cord (and brainstem in bulbar polio).","Lower motor neuron lesion: flaccid, asymmetric, patchy paralysis, areflexia, atrophy; sensation and bladder spared.","Lower limbs more than upper; quadriceps, tibialis anterior, glutei and deltoid commonly affected."]},
    {h:"Stages and prevention", p:["Stages: acute (fever, meningism, paralysis, up to about 2 weeks), convalescent (recovery for up to about 2 years) and residual (permanent paralysis and deformity).","Prevention: oral polio vaccine (OPV) and injectable inactivated polio vaccine (IPV) under the national immunisation schedule; Pulse Polio campaigns; acute flaccid paralysis surveillance.","India was certified polio-free by WHO in 2014."]},
    {h:"Residual paralysis and deformities", p:["Muscle imbalance plus gravity, posture and growth cause deformities.","Hip flexion-abduction-external rotation contracture (tight iliotibial band), knee flexion and genu recurvatum, equinus, cavus, calcaneus, equinovarus or valgus foot.","Shortening of limb, scoliosis, shoulder subluxation from deltoid paralysis.","Hand-to-knee gait from quadriceps weakness; post-polio syndrome (new weakness and fatigue decades later)."]},
    {h:"Orthopaedic management", p:["Acute: rest, positioning, splints to prevent deformity, no injections, pain relief.","Convalescent: muscle charting, splints and calipers (KAFO), gentle stretching.","Residual: soft tissue release (Soutter or Yount release of hip and iliotibial band, tendo-Achilles lengthening), tendon transfers, osteotomies (supracondylar for knee flexion), arthrodesis (triple arthrodesis of foot after 12 years), limb lengthening."]},
    {h:"Principles of muscle (tendon) transfer", p:["Donor muscle must be at least grade 4 (expect loss of one grade), expendable and of similar excursion and power.","Joint must be supple with full passive range (correct contracture first), and transfer should pass in a straight line.","Prefer synergistic transfer; patient must be cooperative and old enough for re-education.","Examples: tibialis posterior to dorsum of foot for foot drop; peroneus longus to os calcis for calcaneus; hamstrings to patella for quadriceps paralysis; trapezius transfer for deltoid paralysis."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["Acute: positioning, gentle passive ROM, splints; avoid fatigue.","Convalescent and residual: strengthening of weak muscles within limits, stretching of tight structures, caliper and gait training, hydrotherapy.","After tendon transfer: immobilise about 4-6 weeks, then re-educate the transferred muscle with active assisted movement, biofeedback or electrical stimulation, functional use and gait.","Post-polio syndrome: pacing and energy conservation; avoid overwork weakness."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Vaccination has eliminated new cases, but residual polio needs balanced orthopaedic correction and focused re-education to maximise walking and independence."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a cross-section of the spinal cord shading the anterior horn as the site of damage. Next to it draw a stick child with hip flexion-abduction, knee flexion and equinus to show common deformities, and an arrow showing tibialis posterior moving to the dorsum of the foot.",
  evidence:[["OPV and IPV vaccination","Eradication of wild polio in most countries","High"],["Tendon transfer for foot drop","Restores active dorsiflexion in selected patients","Low to moderate"],["Pacing and moderate exercise in post-polio syndrome","Improves function without harm","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"WHO polio eradication reports and orthopaedic texts on paralytic deformities",
  outcomes:[["Muscle charting (MRC)","Residual strength"],["Goniometry","Contracture"],["Gait analysis","Walking pattern"],["Leg length","Shortening"]],
  recent:["India has remained polio-free since WHO certification in 2014; the schedule now includes fractional IPV doses."],
  mnemonic:{word:"FAST", items:[["F","Flaccid paralysis"],["A","Asymmetric, Anterior horn"],["S","Sensation spared"],["T","Transfer donor needs grade 4 or more"]], extra:"Correct contracture before transfer."},
  example:"A 24-year-old man in Vellore had polio at age 2 and walks with hand-to-knee gait and foot drop. After tendo-Achilles lengthening and tibialis posterior transfer, physio re-educates dorsiflexion and fits him with a light KAFO.",
  flags:["Acute flaccid paralysis in a child must be notified and investigated","Bulbar weakness or breathing difficulty in acute polio","Avoid injections and strenuous exercise during acute phase","New progressive weakness decades later (post-polio syndrome)"],
  viva:[["Which part of the spinal cord does polio affect?","Anterior horn cells."],["Minimum grade of donor muscle for transfer?","Grade 4 or more, since about one grade is lost after transfer."],["Name a transfer for foot drop.","Tibialis posterior to the dorsum of the foot."]],
  refs:[{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Poliomyelitis."},{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Poliomyelitis"},{b:"park",ch:"Poliomyelitis"}],
  keywords:"poliomyelitis polio residual paralysis deformity tendon transfer anterior horn vaccination",
  mcq:[["In poliomyelitis, which is preserved?",["Muscle power","Deep tendon reflexes","Sensation","Muscle bulk"],2,"Only motor anterior horn cells are destroyed, so sensation is normal."],["Before tendon transfer the joint must have:",["Fixed contracture","Full passive range","Arthrodesis","Ankylosis"],1,"A transferred tendon cannot overcome a fixed contracture."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-limb-deficiencies", title:"Congenital limb deficiencies: radial club hand, proximal femoral focal deficiency, tibial and fibular hemimelia", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Upper and lower limb", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["radial club hand","radial dysplasia","PFFD","proximal femoral focal deficiency","fibular hemimelia","tibial hemimelia","congenital limb deficiency"],
  simple:"Some babies are born with part of a limb bone missing or underdeveloped. In radial club hand the radius is missing so the hand bends towards the thumb side. In the leg, the femur, tibia or fibula may be short or absent, making the limb short and the foot deformed. Treatment ranges from stretching and splints to lengthening, reconstruction, or amputation with a prosthesis.",
  flow:["Failure of limb bud formation in early pregnancy","Absent or short radius, femur, tibia or fibula","Deviated hand, short limb, unstable knee or ankle","Clinical exam, X-ray, check associated anomalies","Stretching, splints, centralisation, lengthening or amputation","Prosthetic or orthotic training, function and gait"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definition and classification", p:["Congenital absence or hypoplasia of all or part of a limb, arising in weeks 4-8 of embryonic life.","ISPO classification: transverse deficiency (like an amputation) and longitudinal deficiency (reduction along the long axis, e.g. radial or fibular).","Older terms: amelia (absent limb), phocomelia (hand or foot attached close to trunk), hemimelia (absent distal part)."]},
    {h:"Radial club hand (radial longitudinal deficiency)", p:["Partial or complete absence of the radius; hand deviated radially, forearm short and bowed, thumb hypoplastic or absent.","Associations: TAR syndrome (thrombocytopenia), Fanconi anaemia, Holt-Oram (heart), VACTERL.","Management: early passive stretching and serial splints; centralisation or radialisation of carpus over ulna around 6-12 months; pollicisation of index finger for absent thumb; ulnar lengthening.","Avoid surgery if elbow is stiff in extension, since radial deviation helps the hand reach the mouth."]},
    {h:"Proximal femoral focal deficiency (PFFD)", p:["Short, flexed, abducted and externally rotated thigh, absent or deficient proximal femur; often with fibular hemimelia.","Aitken classification A-D by presence of femoral head and acetabulum.","Treatment: extension prosthesis; Van Nes rotationplasty (ankle works as knee) or knee arthrodesis with Syme amputation; lengthening in mild cases."]},
    {h:"Fibular and tibial hemimelia", p:["Fibular hemimelia (commonest long bone deficiency): short leg, anterior bowing with skin dimple, equinovalgus foot, missing lateral rays, ACL deficiency. Treatment: lengthening (Ilizarov) for mild shortening; Syme or Boyd amputation with prosthesis for severe.","Tibial hemimelia (rare): short leg, equinovarus foot, unstable knee if proximal tibia absent. Treatment: knee disarticulation and prosthesis if no extensor mechanism; Brown procedure (fibula centralised) or tibiofibular synostosis in partial cases."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["Infancy: parent education, gentle stretching, serial splinting, developmental stimulation.","After surgery: ROM and strengthening, pin site care and joint mobility during lengthening (prevent knee flexion and equinus contracture).","Prosthetic training: fit at pull-to-stand age (about 9-12 months), balance, gait, refit with growth; upper limb training for bimanual function.","Psychosocial support and school adaptations."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["The goal is a functional, painless limb with equal length or a good prosthesis. Early family-centred planning and timely prosthetic fitting give best results."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a forearm with only the ulna and the hand deviated towards the radial side for radial club hand. For the leg, draw a short femur (PFFD) and a short tibia with absent fibula and equinovalgus foot.",
  evidence:[["Centralisation for radial club hand","Improves appearance and hand position; recurrence common","Low"],["Amputation and prosthesis versus lengthening in severe fibular hemimelia","Both give good function; choice individualised","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"Paediatric orthopaedic texts and case series",
  outcomes:[["Leg length discrepancy","Predicted shortening at maturity"],["Hand function tests","Grasp and bimanual use"],["Gait analysis","Prosthetic walking"],["PEDI","Paediatric function"]],
  recent:["Modern external fixators and growth prediction tables allow staged lengthening in milder deficiencies."],
  mnemonic:{word:"RAFT", items:[["R","Radial club hand"],["A","Absent proximal femur (PFFD)"],["F","Fibular hemimelia: commonest"],["T","Tibial hemimelia: rare, varus foot"]], extra:"Transverse vs longitudinal deficiency."},
  example:"A 1-year-old girl in Thanjavur has a short right leg, a skin dimple over the shin and an equinovalgus foot with three toes. Fibular hemimelia is diagnosed; after Syme amputation she is fitted with a prosthesis and starts walking with physio support.",
  flags:["Radial club hand: check platelets, blood count and heart","Severe limb length discrepancy predicted at maturity","Skin breakdown under prosthesis or splint","Neurovascular changes during limb lengthening"],
  viva:[["Commonest congenital long bone deficiency?","Fibular hemimelia."],["What is centralisation?","Placing the carpus over the distal ulna to correct radial club hand."],["What is rotationplasty?","Rotating the leg 180 degrees so the ankle acts as a knee within a prosthesis."]],
  refs:[{b:"campbell",ch:"Limb deficiencies"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Congenital anomalies."},{b:"tecklin",ch:"Limb deficiencies and amputations"}],
  keywords:"radial club hand PFFD fibular hemimelia tibial hemimelia congenital limb deficiency",
  mcq:[["In radial club hand, the hand is deviated towards the:",["Ulnar side","Radial side","Dorsal side","Palmar only"],1,"Absent radius leaves the hand unsupported on the radial side."],["Fibular hemimelia is commonly associated with:",["Equinovarus foot","Equinovalgus foot and absent lateral rays","Calcaneus foot","Pes cavus only"],1,"Absent fibula leads to valgus foot and missing lateral toes."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-mmc-amc-oi", title:"Meningomyelocele, arthrogryposis multiplex congenita and osteogenesis imperfecta", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"General", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["meningomyelocele","myelomeningocele orthopaedic","arthrogryposis","AMC","osteogenesis imperfecta","brittle bone disease","neural tube defect"],
  simple:"These three are childhood conditions seen in orthopaedic clinics. In meningomyelocele the spinal cord and coverings bulge out of the back, causing leg paralysis and loss of sensation below a level. In arthrogryposis babies are born with many stiff joints. In osteogenesis imperfecta a faulty collagen makes bones break easily. Each needs careful handling, splinting and orthopaedic surgery planned around function.",
  flow:["Neural tube defect, fetal akinesia, or type I collagen mutation","Paralysis below level; fixed joints; fragile bones","Deformities, contractures, recurrent fractures","Neurological level, ROM, fracture history, X-ray","Closure and shunt, casting and release, rodding and bisphosphonates","Orthoses, positioning, safe handling, mobility training"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Meningomyelocele", p:["Neural tube defect where meninges and neural tissue protrude through a vertebral arch defect, usually lumbosacral; folic acid before conception reduces risk.","Features: flaccid paralysis and sensory loss below the level, neurogenic bladder and bowel, hydrocephalus (Arnold-Chiari II), latex allergy.","Orthopaedic problems: hip dislocation, knee flexion or extension contracture, clubfoot, vertical talus, scoliosis and kyphosis, pressure sores, fractures in insensate limbs.","Management: closure within 48 hours, VP shunt, clean intermittent catheterisation; soft tissue releases, aim for a plantigrade foot and straight spine; walking potential depends on level (L4 and below often community walkers)."]},
    {h:"Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita", p:["Non-progressive congenital stiffness of two or more joints due to reduced fetal movement (neurogenic, myopathic, or crowding).","Amyoplasia (classic type): symmetrical, internally rotated shoulders, extended elbows, flexed wrists, clubfeet, hip dislocation; tubular limbs without creases, normal intelligence and sensation.","Management: early stretching and serial casting, splints; surgery for clubfoot, hips and knees; elbow release or tendon transfer for feeding; wrist osteotomy."]},
    {h:"Osteogenesis imperfecta", p:["Genetic defect of type I collagen (usually autosomal dominant); Sillence types I-IV, type II lethal.","Features: fragile bones with multiple fractures from minor trauma, blue sclera, dentinogenesis imperfecta, hearing loss, short stature, ligament laxity, bowed long bones, scoliosis.","Investigations: X-ray shows thin cortices, osteopenia, multiple healing fractures; genetic testing.","Management: bisphosphonates (pamidronate, zoledronate) to reduce fractures; intramedullary telescoping rods (Fassier-Duval) with osteotomies to correct bowing; scoliosis surgery."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["Meningomyelocele: positioning, ROM, strengthening of innervated muscles, standing frames, orthoses (RGO, HKAFO, KAFO, AFO depending on level), wheelchair skills, skin inspection.","AMC: frequent gentle stretching (several times a day), serial splints, functional training and adaptive devices for feeding and writing.","OI: safe handling (support trunk and limbs, never lift by ankles or pull on limbs), hydrotherapy, active exercise, protected standing and walking with orthoses, fracture-safe play."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Goals are functional mobility, prevention of deformity and protection of skin and bone. Team care with neurosurgery, urology, orthopaedics and therapy gives the best outcome."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a sagittal sketch of a lumbar meningomyelocele sac containing neural tissue. Next to it draw a child with extended elbows and internally rotated shoulders (AMC) and a bowed femur with an intramedullary rod (OI).",
  evidence:[["Periconceptional folic acid","Reduces neural tube defects","High"],["Bisphosphonates in children with OI","Increase bone density; fracture reduction less certain","Moderate"],["Early serial casting in AMC","Improves joint position","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane reviews on folic acid and bisphosphonates in OI; paediatric orthopaedic texts",
  outcomes:[["Hoffer ambulation level","Walking ability in spina bifida"],["Goniometry","Contractures"],["Fracture frequency","OI bone fragility"],["PEDI or WeeFIM","Function"]],
  recent:["Fetal surgery for myelomeningocele (MOMS trial) reduced shunt need and improved motor outcome in selected cases."],
  mnemonic:{word:"MAO", items:[["M","Meningomyelocele: paralysis plus sensory loss"],["A","AMC: many stiff joints, normal sensation"],["O","OI: brittle bones, blue sclera"]], extra:"Never lift an OI child by the legs."},
  example:"A 3-year-old boy in Erode with L4 level meningomyelocele has a dislocated hip and bilateral clubfeet. After foot correction he uses AFOs with a walker, and his mother checks his insensate feet for sores daily.",
  flags:["Sudden change in function or headache in shunted child (shunt block)","Painless swelling of an insensate limb (fracture)","Pressure sores in anaesthetic skin","Rough handling in OI causing fractures"],
  viva:[["What is amyoplasia?","The classic symmetrical type of arthrogryposis with replacement of muscle by fibrous and fatty tissue."],["Collagen affected in OI?","Type I collagen."],["Which vitamin prevents neural tube defects?","Folic acid taken before and in early pregnancy."]],
  refs:[{b:"campbell",ch:"Myelodysplasia, arthrogryposis and osteogenesis imperfecta"},{b:"tecklin",ch:"Spina bifida and musculoskeletal disorders"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Congenital and developmental disorders."}],
  keywords:"meningomyelocele arthrogryposis osteogenesis imperfecta brittle bone spina bifida AMC",
  mcq:[["Blue sclera and multiple fractures suggest:",["Arthrogryposis","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Rickets","Meningomyelocele"],1,"Defective type I collagen causes blue sclera and fragile bones."],["In arthrogryposis multiplex congenita, sensation is usually:",["Absent below a level","Patchy","Normal","Lost in a glove distribution"],2,"Joint stiffness is the problem; sensation and intelligence are usually normal."]]
}
