{
  id:"co-upper-limb-nerve-injuries", title:"Radial, median and ulnar nerve lesions", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Upper limb", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["wrist drop","radial nerve palsy","median nerve injury","ape thumb","ulnar claw hand","Saturday night palsy","Froment sign"],
  simple:"Three main nerves run down the arm. If the radial nerve is cut, the wrist drops because the extensors stop working. If the median nerve is injured, the thumb cannot move across the palm and the thumb side of the hand goes numb. If the ulnar nerve is injured, the small hand muscles waste and the ring and little fingers claw. Splints keep the hand in a good position while the nerve regrows.",
  flow:["Fracture, laceration, compression or entrapment of nerve","Neurapraxia, axonotmesis or neurotmesis","Wrist drop, ape thumb or claw hand with sensory loss","Motor and sensory testing, Tinel sign, NCS and EMG","Observe, repair, graft or tendon transfer; splint","Splinting, sensory and motor re-education, function"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Classification of nerve injury", p:["Seddon: neurapraxia (conduction block, recovers in weeks), axonotmesis (axon divided, sheath intact, regrows about 1 mm a day), neurotmesis (nerve divided, needs surgery).","Sunderland grades 1-5 add detail by layer injured.","Tinel sign advancing along the nerve indicates regeneration."]},
    {h:"Radial nerve", p:["Causes: fracture shaft of humerus (spiral groove), Saturday night palsy, crutch palsy (axilla), posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) injury at the elbow (Monteggia, Frohse arcade).","High lesion (axilla): triceps also weak; spiral groove: triceps spared, wrist drop, finger and thumb drop, sensory loss in first dorsal web space.","PIN palsy: finger drop without wrist drop (ECRL spared), no sensory loss.","Splint: cock-up or dynamic radial palsy splint. Tendon transfer if no recovery: pronator teres to ECRB, FCU or FCR to EDC, palmaris longus to EPL."]},
    {h:"Median nerve", p:["Causes: supracondylar fracture, wrist lacerations, carpal tunnel syndrome, lunate dislocation.","High lesion: loss of forearm pronation, wrist flexion weak with ulnar deviation, index cannot flex (pointing index, Ochsner clasp test, benediction attitude on attempting a fist).","Low lesion (wrist): thenar wasting, loss of opposition and abduction (ape thumb, pen test), sensory loss over lateral 3 and half digits on palmar side; 'eye of the hand' lost.","Anterior interosseous nerve: cannot make an O sign (FPL and FDP index weak), no sensory loss.","Opponens splint; opponensplasty (ring FDS or EIP) if no recovery."]},
    {h:"Ulnar nerve", p:["Causes: elbow fracture or cubitus valgus (tardy ulnar palsy), cubital tunnel, wrist laceration, Guyon canal compression, leprosy.","Claw hand of ring and little fingers (MCP hyperextension, IP flexion), interossei and hypothenar wasting, Card test and Froment sign (thumb IP flexes when pinching paper), Wartenberg sign, sensory loss over medial 1 and half fingers.","Ulnar paradox: low lesion claws more than high lesion because FDP is still working.","Knuckle-bender splint; tendon transfer (Zancolli lasso, Bunnell) for claw."]},
    {h:"Orthopaedic management", p:["Closed injury: observe 6-12 weeks with splint, NCS at about 3 weeks; explore if no recovery.","Open cut: primary repair within days if clean; nerve graft for gaps.","Late cases or poor recovery: tendon transfers and arthrodesis."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["Splint to prevent deformity and support function; passive ROM to keep joints supple.","Electrical stimulation of denervated muscle (interrupted galvanic, long pulse) to slow atrophy is optional and evidence is limited; protect anaesthetic skin.","Motor re-education when reinnervation starts (MRC grade 1-2): facilitation, biofeedback, progressive resisted exercise.","Sensory re-education and desensitisation; tendon transfer re-education; functional hand activities."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw three hands side by side: wrist drop (radial), ape thumb with benediction (median), and claw of ring and little fingers (ulnar). Shade the sensory loss area for each and label the classic sign under it.",
  evidence:[["Early microsurgical repair of divided nerves","Better recovery than delayed repair","Moderate"],["Sensory re-education after nerve repair","Improves sensory function","Low to moderate"],["Electrical stimulation of denervated muscle","Mixed evidence for recovery","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"Hand surgery texts and systematic reviews of nerve rehabilitation",
  outcomes:[["MRC motor and sensory grades","Nerve recovery"],["Tinel sign progression","Regeneration"],["Two-point discrimination","Sensory recovery"],["DASH","Upper limb function"]],
  recent:["Nerve transfers (moving a working nerve branch to a paralysed muscle) are increasingly used alongside tendon transfers."],
  mnemonic:{word:"DAC", items:[["D","Drop wrist: radial"],["A","Ape thumb: median"],["C","Claw hand: ulnar"]], extra:"Froment sign = ulnar; O sign failure = AIN."},
  example:"A 28-year-old construction worker in Chennai has a fracture of the shaft of humerus and cannot lift his wrist. A cock-up splint is given, passive ROM is maintained and by 10 weeks wrist extension starts returning.",
  flags:["Complete nerve loss after open wound (explore and repair)","Nerve palsy that appears after manipulation of fracture","No recovery by 3-4 months in closed injury","Burns or ulcers in anaesthetic skin"],
  viva:[["Nerve injured in fracture shaft of humerus?","Radial nerve in the spiral groove."],["What is Froment sign?","Flexion of the thumb IP joint when pinching paper, showing adductor pollicis weakness in ulnar palsy."],["Why is the median nerve called the eye of the hand?","It supplies sensation to the main gripping and pinching surfaces of the hand."]],
  refs:[{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Peripheral nerve injuries."},{b:"magee",ch:"Forearm, wrist and hand"},{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Peripheral nerve injuries"}],
  keywords:"radial nerve median nerve ulnar nerve wrist drop ape thumb claw hand Froment Seddon",
  mcq:[["Wrist drop with intact triceps indicates radial nerve lesion at:",["Axilla","Spiral groove","Wrist","Fingers"],1,"Branches to triceps leave above the spiral groove."],["Froment sign tests the:",["Opponens pollicis","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus"],1,"FPL substitutes for a weak adductor pollicis in ulnar palsy."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-sciatic-peroneal-palsy", title:"Sciatic nerve and lateral popliteal (common peroneal) nerve lesions", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Lower limb", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["sciatic nerve injury","common peroneal nerve palsy","lateral popliteal nerve palsy","foot drop","high stepping gait","injection palsy"],
  simple:"The sciatic nerve is the big nerve at the back of the thigh. It divides into the tibial nerve and the common peroneal (lateral popliteal) nerve. The common peroneal nerve winds around the neck of the fibula where it is easily pressed or injured, causing foot drop. An ankle-foot orthosis helps walking while the nerve recovers.",
  flow:["Hip dislocation, injection, fibula fracture, tight plaster, crossed legs","Sciatic or peroneal nerve conduction lost","Foot drop, high stepping gait, sensory loss","Muscle testing, sensation, Tinel, NCS and EMG","Remove compression, repair or graft, tendon transfer","AFO, stretching, re-education, gait training"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Anatomy", p:["Sciatic nerve (L4-S3) leaves the pelvis below piriformis, supplies hamstrings and adductor magnus, and divides near the popliteal fossa.","Tibial nerve: calf, sole muscles; sensation of sole.","Common peroneal (lateral popliteal) nerve winds round the fibular neck: deep peroneal (dorsiflexors, first web space sensation) and superficial peroneal (evertors, dorsum of foot sensation)."]},
    {h:"Sciatic nerve lesion", p:["Causes: posterior dislocation of hip, acetabular fracture, THR, misplaced gluteal injection (common in India, especially in children), pelvic tumours, penetrating wounds.","Features: weak knee flexion (high lesion), complete paralysis below the knee, foot drop, loss of ankle reflex, sensory loss below the knee except medial leg (saphenous), trophic ulcers of sole.","Peroneal division is usually more affected than tibial division."]},
    {h:"Common peroneal nerve lesion", p:["Causes: fracture of fibular neck, knee dislocation or lateral ligament injury, tight plaster or bandage, prolonged leg crossing, squatting, weight loss, leprosy, compartment syndrome.","Features: foot drop with inversion (loss of dorsiflexion and eversion), high stepping gait (steppage), sensory loss over the lateral leg and dorsum of foot.","Tibialis posterior and calf are normal, distinguishing it from L5 radiculopathy (where tibialis posterior and hip abductors are also weak)."]},
    {h:"Investigations and orthopaedic management", p:["NCS and EMG after 3 weeks; MRI or ultrasound for compression or tumour.","Closed compression: remove the cause, observe with splint for 3-4 months.","Open injury: primary repair or nerve grafting; neurolysis for entrapment.","Persistent foot drop: tibialis posterior transfer through the interosseous membrane to the dorsum of foot; triple arthrodesis for late fixed deformity."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["AFO (posterior leaf spring or foot drop splint) to correct gait and prevent equinus.","Passive ROM and calf stretching to prevent contracture.","Electrical stimulation of dorsiflexors (optional) and functional electrical stimulation peroneal devices for gait.","Re-education as power returns: active assisted dorsiflexion, eversion, progressive resistance, balance and gait training.","Skin care and footwear for anaesthetic sole after sciatic injury."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Most compressive peroneal palsies recover if the cause is removed early. A well-fitted AFO and preventing contracture keep the patient walking safely during recovery."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw the knee from the lateral side with the common peroneal nerve winding around the fibular neck and dividing into deep and superficial branches. Shade the sensory loss on the dorsum of the foot and show a high stepping gait stick figure.",
  evidence:[["AFO for foot drop","Improves walking speed and safety","Moderate"],["Tibialis posterior transfer for permanent foot drop","Restores active dorsiflexion in many patients","Low"],["Peroneal FES devices","Similar walking benefit to AFO in neurological foot drop","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Orthopaedic texts and trials of FES versus AFO",
  outcomes:[["MRC grade of dorsiflexors","Motor recovery"],["10-metre walk test","Walking speed"],["Sensory mapping","Sensory recovery"],["Gait analysis","Foot clearance"]],
  recent:["Safe injection practice (vastus lateralis or ventrogluteal site) is promoted to prevent sciatic injection palsy."],
  mnemonic:{word:"PED", items:[["P","Peroneal"],["E","Eversion lost"],["D","Dorsiflexion lost"]], extra:"TIP: Tibial nerve gives Inversion and Plantarflexion."},
  example:"A 22-year-old college student in Coimbatore develops foot drop after a below-knee plaster for an ankle injury. The tight cast is removed, he is given a posterior leaf spring AFO and his dorsiflexion recovers over 3 months.",
  flags:["Foot drop with back pain and sensory loss in a dermatome (L5 radiculopathy)","Foot drop with tense swollen leg (compartment syndrome)","Bilateral foot drop (neuropathy or cauda equina)","Anaesthetic sole ulcers"],
  viva:[["Where is the common peroneal nerve most vulnerable?","At the neck of the fibula."],["Gait in foot drop?","High stepping (steppage) gait."],["Common cause of sciatic injury in children in India?","Wrongly placed gluteal injection."]],
  refs:[{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Peripheral nerve injuries."},{b:"magee",ch:"Lower leg, ankle and foot"},{b:"osullivan",ch:"Orthotics: ankle-foot orthoses"}],
  keywords:"sciatic nerve common peroneal lateral popliteal foot drop AFO high stepping injection palsy",
  mcq:[["Common peroneal nerve palsy causes loss of:",["Plantarflexion and inversion","Dorsiflexion and eversion","Knee flexion","Hip abduction"],1,"Deep and superficial peroneal branches supply dorsiflexors and evertors."],["The splint used for foot drop is:",["Cock-up splint","Knuckle-bender","Ankle-foot orthosis","Thomas splint"],2,"An AFO holds the ankle in neutral to clear the toes in swing."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-hand-injuries", title:"Hand injuries: tendon, bone and joint injuries (mallet finger, boutonniere, metacarpal and phalangeal fractures, Bennett fracture, gamekeeper thumb)", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"Hand", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["hand injuries","mallet finger","boxer fracture","Bennett fracture","gamekeeper thumb","extensor tendon injury","flexor tendon zones"],
  simple:"The hand has many small bones, tendons and joints packed tightly together, so even small injuries can leave it stiff. Tendons can be cut or pulled off, bones can break, and joints can dislocate or tear their ligaments. Good treatment holds the hand in a safe position for a short time and then moves it early. Physiotherapy and splints decide how well the hand works afterwards.",
  flow:["Cuts, crush, ball hitting fingertip, punching, falls","Tendon division, avulsion, fracture or ligament tear","Loss of active movement, deformity, swelling","Test each tendon, X-ray, check nerves and vessels","Repair, splint in safe position, fix fractures","Early protected motion, oedema control, strengthening"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Principles", p:["Hand is splinted in the safe (intrinsic plus, James) position: wrist about 20-30 degrees extension, MCP 70-90 degrees flexion, IP joints in extension, to keep collateral ligaments long.","Elevation and early motion prevent oedema and stiffness; immobilise only the injured ray and for the shortest time.","Always check rotation of fingers (scissoring) in fractures and test FDS and FDP separately."]},
    {h:"Tendon injuries", p:["Flexor tendons: Verdan zones 1-5; zone 2 (no man's land, within the fibro-osseous sheath) has the worst results; primary repair with multi-strand core suture and early protected mobilisation (modified Kleinert, Duran or early active motion).","Extensor tendons: zones 1-8; mallet finger (zone 1, avulsion of extensor from distal phalanx) treated with DIP extension splint for 6-8 weeks full time; boutonniere (zone 3, central slip) PIP extension splint 6 weeks with DIP free.","Jersey finger: FDP avulsion from ring finger in rugby or kabaddi grab; early surgical reattachment."]},
    {h:"Bone injuries", p:["Metacarpal neck fracture of little finger (boxer fracture): accept some angulation, buddy strap or ulnar gutter splint; fix if rotated.","Metacarpal shaft and phalangeal fractures: splint, K-wires or plates if unstable or rotated.","Bennett fracture: intra-articular fracture-dislocation of the base of the first metacarpal; APL pulls the shaft; needs reduction and K-wire or screw. Rolando fracture: comminuted T or Y pattern.","Scaphoid fracture: see separate topic (risk of avascular necrosis)."]},
    {h:"Joint injuries", p:["PIP dislocation (usually dorsal) and volar plate injury: reduce, short extension block splint, early motion.","Gamekeeper (skier) thumb: ulnar collateral ligament tear of thumb MCP; partial tear thumb spica 4-6 weeks; complete tear with Stener lesion needs surgical repair.","MCP and CMC dislocations: reduction and stabilisation."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["Oedema control: elevation, retrograde massage, compression gloves, active fist pumping of free fingers.","Protected motion per protocol; tendon gliding exercises; blocking exercises for FDS and FDP.","Scar management: massage, silicone, pressure; desensitisation.","Joint mobilisation and dynamic or static progressive splints for stiffness after healing; grip and pinch strengthening from about 8-12 weeks; work-specific training."]},
    {h:"Complications and conclusion", p:["Stiffness, tendon adhesions, rupture of repair, malunion with rotation, CRPS, swan neck after untreated mallet.","Correct splint position and early, controlled movement are the keys to a functional hand."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw an outline of the hand with flexor zones 1-5 marked, highlighting zone 2 as no man's land. Add a side view of a finger showing mallet finger (DIP drooping) and a sketch of the safe splint position.",
  evidence:[["Early active motion after flexor tendon repair","Better range with acceptable rupture rates","Moderate"],["Splint for mallet finger","Most heal with continuous splinting; type of splint matters less than compliance","Moderate"],["Buddy strapping for stable boxer fracture","Function similar to casting","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane reviews on mallet finger and metacarpal fractures; hand therapy literature",
  outcomes:[["Total active motion (TAM)","Finger range"],["Grip and pinch dynamometry","Strength"],["DASH or Michigan Hand Questionnaire","Function"],["Strickland criteria","Flexor tendon repair results"]],
  recent:["WALANT (wide awake local anaesthesia no tourniquet) surgery lets surgeons check tendon repair glide during the operation."],
  mnemonic:{word:"SAFE", items:[["S","Splint wrist in extension"],["A","Angle MCP 70-90 flexion"],["F","Fingers IP straight"],["E","Elevate and move Early"]], extra:"Zone 2 = no man's land."},
  example:"A 19-year-old cricketer in Chennai cannot straighten his fingertip after the ball hit it. X-ray shows no fracture; he wears a DIP extension splint full time for 8 weeks and is taught to keep it straight while changing the tape.",
  flags:["Rotational malalignment of a finger","Fight bite over knuckle (risk of septic arthritis)","Numb or pale fingertip after injury","Flexor sheath infection signs (Kanavel signs)"],
  viva:[["What is Bennett fracture?","Intra-articular fracture-dislocation at the base of the first metacarpal."],["Which flexor zone is called no man's land?","Zone 2, inside the digital fibro-osseous sheath."],["What is a Stener lesion?","Adductor aponeurosis trapped between the torn thumb UCL and its insertion, preventing healing."]],
  refs:[{b:"brotzman",ch:"Hand and wrist injuries"},{b:"magee",ch:"Forearm, wrist and hand"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Injuries of the hand."}],
  keywords:"hand injury mallet finger Bennett fracture boxer fracture gamekeeper thumb tendon zones safe position",
  mcq:[["Mallet finger is managed conservatively by splinting the:",["PIP in flexion","DIP in extension","MCP in extension","Wrist in flexion"],1,"DIP is held straight continuously for 6-8 weeks."],["In the safe position of hand splinting, the MCP joints are kept in:",["Full extension","Hyperextension","70-90 degrees flexion","Neutral rotation only"],2,"MCP flexion keeps the collateral ligaments at full length."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-leprosy", title:"Leprosy (Hansen disease): neuritis, paralysis, trophic ulcers and deformities", subject:"Musculoskeletal", region:"General", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["leprosy","Hansen disease","leprosy claw hand","trophic ulcer","lagophthalmos","leprosy foot drop","leprosy neuritis"],
  simple:"Leprosy is a slow bacterial infection that likes cool nerves near the skin. Damaged nerves cause patches of numb skin, weak muscles and dry skin. Because the hands and feet cannot feel pain, small injuries become ulcers and fingers slowly shorten. Multidrug therapy cures the infection, and physiotherapy, splints, footwear and self-care prevent disability.",
  flow:["Mycobacterium leprae spread by droplets","Invades Schwann cells of peripheral nerves","Anaesthetic patches, thick nerves, paralysis, dry skin","Sensory testing, nerve palpation, VMT, slit skin smear","MDT, steroids for neuritis, surgery for deformity","Splints, exercises, footwear, self-care, tendon transfer rehab"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Pathology and types", p:["Chronic granulomatous infection by Mycobacterium leprae, affecting skin and peripheral nerves, especially where they are superficial and cool.","Ridley-Jopling spectrum: tuberculoid (TT), borderline (BT, BB, BL), lepromatous (LL); WHO groups paucibacillary (up to 5 patches) and multibacillary (more than 5 patches or nerve involvement).","Lepra reactions: type 1 (reversal, acute neuritis) and type 2 (erythema nodosum leprosum); both can rapidly damage nerves."]},
    {h:"Neuritis and paralysis", p:["Nerves commonly thickened: ulnar (above elbow), median (wrist), common peroneal (fibular neck), posterior tibial (ankle), facial, great auricular, radial cutaneous.","Ulnar: claw of ring and little fingers; ulnar plus median: complete claw hand with ape thumb; radial (rare): wrist drop.","Common peroneal: foot drop; posterior tibial: claw toes and anaesthetic sole.","Facial nerve: lagophthalmos (inability to close eye) leading to corneal ulcer."]},
    {h:"Trophic ulcers and deformities", p:["Primary deformities: due to disease itself (nerve paralysis, skin lesions, madarosis, nasal collapse).","Secondary deformities: due to injury of anaesthetic parts: plantar ulcers over metatarsal heads and heel, burns, shortening and absorption of fingers and toes, Charcot joints of foot.","Autonomic damage: dry cracked skin which fissures and gets infected.","WHO disability grading: 0 none, 1 loss of sensation, 2 visible deformity or damage."]},
    {h:"Medical and orthopaedic management", p:["WHO multidrug therapy (rifampicin, dapsone, clofazimine) for 6 months (PB) or 12 months (MB).","Acute neuritis: oral prednisolone course and splinting of the affected limb; nerve decompression if not responding.","Ulcer: rest, no weight bearing, cleaning, plaster cast for plantar ulcer, debridement.","Reconstructive surgery after disease is controlled: tendon transfers (FDS lasso or Zancolli for claw, opponensplasty, tibialis posterior transfer for foot drop, temporalis transfer for lagophthalmos)."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy (short)", p:["Neuritis: resting splints, no exercise to the painful nerve, gentle passive ROM after acute phase.","Prevent contracture: daily stretching of fingers and thumb web, oil massage after soaking dry skin.","Strengthening of weak intrinsics; pre-operative and post-operative re-education of transferred tendons.","Protective footwear (MCR sole), self-care training: daily inspection, soak and oil, use of gloves and cloth when handling hot objects; vision care exercises for lagophthalmos."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Early MDT and prompt treatment of neuritis prevent most deformities; lifelong self-care protects anaesthetic hands and feet, and reconstructive surgery with rehab restores function and reduces stigma."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a body outline marking the commonly thickened nerves (ulnar at elbow, median at wrist, common peroneal at fibula neck, posterior tibial at ankle, facial). Add a sole sketch shading ulcer sites under the metatarsal heads and heel.",
  evidence:[["WHO MDT","Cures infection and reduces transmission","High"],["Corticosteroids for acute neuritis","Improve nerve function in short term","Moderate"],["Self-care and protective footwear","Reduce ulcer recurrence","Low to moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"WHO leprosy guidelines and Cochrane reviews on leprosy neuritis",
  outcomes:[["Voluntary muscle testing (VMT)","Motor nerve function"],["Monofilament sensory testing","Protective sensation"],["WHO disability grade","Impairment level"],["EHF score","Overall eye-hand-foot impairment"]],
  recent:["Single-dose rifampicin post-exposure prophylaxis for contacts is now part of WHO and Indian leprosy programmes."],
  mnemonic:{word:"UMPF", items:[["U","Ulnar at elbow"],["M","Median at wrist"],["P","Peroneal at fibula neck and Posterior tibial at ankle"],["F","Facial nerve"]], extra:"Cool superficial nerves are attacked."},
  example:"A 40-year-old farmer in Villupuram has numb patches and claw ring and little fingers with a painless ulcer under his big toe. He starts MDT, wears MCR footwear, learns soak-and-oil care and later has a lasso tendon transfer with physio re-education.",
  flags:["Painful, tender nerve with new weakness (acute neuritis, urgent steroids)","Inability to close the eye (corneal damage risk)","Hot swollen foot with intact skin (Charcot joint or infection)","Never apply heat or electrotherapy over anaesthetic skin"],
  viva:[["Which nerve is most commonly thickened in leprosy?","Ulnar nerve above the elbow."],["What is a primary deformity?","A deformity caused directly by the disease, such as paralysis or skin infiltration."],["Footwear for anaesthetic feet?","Microcellular rubber (MCR) soled footwear."]],
  refs:[{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Leprosy."},{b:"park",ch:"Leprosy"},{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Peripheral nerve lesions"}],
  keywords:"leprosy Hansen neuritis claw hand trophic ulcer lagophthalmos MDT footwear",
  mcq:[["The commonest site of trophic ulcer in leprosy is:",["Dorsum of foot","Under the metatarsal heads","Calf","Thigh"],1,"Weight bearing on anaesthetic forefoot breaks the skin."],["Lagophthalmos in leprosy is due to involvement of the:",["Optic nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve","Oculomotor nerve"],2,"Orbicularis oculi weakness from facial nerve damage."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-rehab-principles", title:"Rehabilitation: definition, aims and principles", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"General", stages:"steps",
  aliases:["definition of rehabilitation","aims of rehabilitation","principles of rehabilitation","impairment disability handicap","types of rehabilitation","medical rehabilitation"],
  simple:"Rehabilitation helps a person with illness or injury become as independent and active in life as possible. It looks at the whole person, not just the damaged body part. It works best when it starts early, sets goals with the patient, uses a team and continues into the home and community.",
  flow:["Define the person's health condition and needs","Assess impairment, activity limitation and participation","Set realistic goals with patient and family","Team delivers therapy, aids and training","Adapt home, work and community","Review goals and follow up long term"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definition", p:["WHO: rehabilitation is a set of interventions designed to optimise functioning and reduce disability in individuals with health conditions in interaction with their environment.","Older definition: restoration of the handicapped to the fullest physical, mental, social, vocational and economic usefulness of which they are capable.","Habilitation: developing skills never acquired (for example, congenital disability), rehabilitation restores lost skills."]},
    {h:"Concepts", p:["ICIDH (1980): impairment (organ level), disability (person level), handicap (social level).","ICF (2001): body functions and structures, activity, participation, influenced by environmental and personal factors.","Types: medical, vocational, social, psychological and educational rehabilitation."]},
    {h:"Aims", p:["Prevent or reduce impairment and secondary complications (contracture, pressure sore, deconditioning).","Restore maximum function and independence in ADL and mobility.","Provide aids, appliances and environmental modifications.","Return to education, work and social roles; improve quality of life and integrate the person in the community.","Educate patient and family for self-management."]},
    {h:"Principles", p:["Start early, ideally from the acute phase (rehab begins on day one).","Holistic and person-centred: physical, psychological, social and vocational needs.","Team approach with the patient and family as core members.","Goal setting: SMART goals, short and long term, reviewed regularly.","Emphasis on ability, not disability; functional and task-specific training.","Continuity of care from hospital to home and community; cost-effective, accessible services (CBR)."]},
    {h:"Process (steps)", p:["Assessment and problem list (ICF based).","Goal setting and treatment plan.","Intervention: therapy, assistive devices, education, counselling.","Re-assessment with outcome measures and discharge planning.","Follow-up and community reintegration."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Rehabilitation is about function and participation. Early, team-based, goal-oriented care that includes the family and the environment gives the best results."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw the ICF diagram: health condition at the top, three boxes (body structure and function, activity, participation) joined by two-way arrows, and environmental and personal factors at the bottom.",
  evidence:[["Organised multidisciplinary inpatient rehabilitation (e.g. stroke units)","Reduces death and dependency","High"],["Goal setting in rehabilitation","May improve engagement and quality of life","Low to moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"WHO Rehabilitation 2030 and Cochrane reviews on organised stroke care and goal setting",
  outcomes:[["Barthel Index","ADL independence"],["FIM","Burden of care"],["WHODAS 2.0","Disability across ICF domains"],["Goal attainment scaling","Individual goals"]],
  recent:["WHO Rehabilitation 2030 call for action and the 2023 World Health Assembly resolution urge integrating rehabilitation into primary health care."],
  mnemonic:{word:"EARLY", items:[["E","Early start"],["A","Ability focus"],["R","Realistic goals"],["L","Lifelong follow-up in community"],["Y","You plus team (patient-centred team)"]], extra:"ICF: function, activity, participation."},
  example:"A 55-year-old school teacher in Tiruchirappalli has a stroke. From day 2 the team positions him, sets goals with his wife, trains walking and ADL, and later the school adds a ramp so he can return to teaching.",
  flags:["Unrealistic goals that ignore the patient's priorities","Medical instability limiting therapy","Depression reducing participation","Discharge without home assessment or follow-up"],
  viva:[["Difference between habilitation and rehabilitation?","Habilitation develops skills never acquired; rehabilitation restores lost skills."],["Three components of ICIDH?","Impairment, disability and handicap."],["What does ICF stand for?","International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health."]],
  refs:[{c:"World Health Organization. Rehabilitation 2030: a call for action. WHO, 2017."},{b:"osullivan",ch:"Clinical decision making and the rehabilitation process"},{c:"Frontera WR (ed). DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. Chapter: Concepts of rehabilitation."}],
  keywords:"rehabilitation definition aims principles ICF ICIDH habilitation team goals",
  mcq:[["In ICIDH, the social disadvantage resulting from a disability is called:",["Impairment","Activity","Handicap","Participation"],2,"Handicap is the social-level consequence in ICIDH."],["Which is NOT a principle of rehabilitation?",["Early start","Team approach","Focus on disability rather than ability","Goal setting"],2,"Rehab emphasises remaining ability."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-rehab-team", title:"Rehabilitation team and the role of each member", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"General", stages:"steps",
  aliases:["rehabilitation team","multidisciplinary team","interdisciplinary team","role of physiotherapist","role of occupational therapist","team members rehabilitation"],
  simple:"No single professional can meet all the needs of a disabled person. The rehabilitation team brings together the doctor, physiotherapist, occupational therapist, speech therapist, nurse, prosthetist, psychologist, social worker and others, with the patient and family at the centre. Each member brings a skill, and regular meetings keep everyone working towards the same goals.",
  flow:["Patient and family at the centre","Physiatrist or doctor leads medical care","Each member assesses their area","Team meeting sets shared goals","Members deliver coordinated intervention","Review progress and plan discharge"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Team models", p:["Multidisciplinary: members work in parallel, each with own goals, communicating through the leader.","Interdisciplinary: members assess separately but meet to set common goals and plan together (preferred in most rehab units).","Transdisciplinary: members share roles and one primary person delivers most care with others consulting (useful in CBR and early intervention)."]},
    {h:"Core members and roles (1)", p:["Patient and family: central members; share goals, carry out home programme.","Physiatrist (PMR specialist) or treating doctor: diagnosis, medical management, prescription, team leader, coordination.","Physiotherapist: assessment of movement and function, therapeutic exercise, mobility and gait training, electrotherapy, chest care, prevention of complications, aids and home programme.","Occupational therapist: ADL training, upper limb and hand function, splints, adaptive devices, home and work modifications, cognitive and perceptual training."]},
    {h:"Core members and roles (2)", p:["Speech and language therapist: communication disorders, dysphagia assessment and training.","Rehabilitation nurse: 24-hour care, positioning, skin, bladder and bowel training, medication, carry-over of therapy.","Prosthetist and orthotist: design, fabrication, fitting and maintenance of prostheses and orthoses.","Clinical psychologist: cognitive assessment, counselling, behavioural therapy, adjustment to disability."]},
    {h:"Other members", p:["Medical social worker: family counselling, finance, disability certificate and government schemes, community resources.","Vocational counsellor: pre-vocational assessment, job training and placement.","Special educator, recreation therapist, dietician, orthopaedic surgeon, neurologist, urologist, rehabilitation engineer.","Community workers (CBR workers, ASHA) for follow-up at home."]},
    {h:"Team functioning", p:["Regular team conferences with shared problem list and goals.","Clear communication, documentation and respect for each role.","Patient-centred goal setting; avoid duplication or gaps in care.","Barriers: hierarchy, poor communication, role confusion; solved by leadership, joint training and shared records."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Rehabilitation is a team effort with the patient at the centre. Coordinated interdisciplinary work gives better outcomes than separate professionals working alone."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a wheel with the patient and family in the hub and each team member as a spoke (doctor, PT, OT, speech therapist, nurse, P and O, psychologist, social worker, vocational counsellor), labelling one key role on each spoke.",
  evidence:[["Coordinated multidisciplinary rehabilitation after stroke","Better survival and independence","High"],["Interdisciplinary rehab for chronic low back pain","Improves pain and function more than usual care","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane reviews on organised stroke unit care and multidisciplinary biopsychosocial rehabilitation",
  outcomes:[["FIM","Team-level functional outcome"],["Length of stay","Efficiency"],["Goal attainment","Team goal achievement"],["Patient satisfaction","Experience of care"]],
  recent:["The Rehabilitation Council of India and National Commission for Allied and Healthcare Professions (NCAHP) Act 2021 regulate many rehab team professions in India."],
  mnemonic:{word:"PPOSN", items:[["P","Physiatrist leads"],["P","Physiotherapist moves"],["O","Occupational therapist for ADL"],["S","Speech therapist and Social worker"],["N","Nurse for 24-hour care"]], extra:"Patient and family at the centre."},
  example:"A 30-year-old lorry driver in Namakkal with paraplegia is managed by a team: the physiatrist leads, the PT trains transfers and wheelchair skills, the nurse manages bladder, the OT adapts his home, and the social worker helps him get a disability certificate and loan.",
  flags:["Conflicting advice from team members","Patient excluded from goal setting","No discharge or community follow-up plan","Caregiver burnout unrecognised"],
  viva:[["Who is the most important member of the rehab team?","The patient, along with the family."],["Difference between multidisciplinary and interdisciplinary teams?","Multidisciplinary members work in parallel; interdisciplinary members set shared goals together."],["Role of a prosthetist?","Design, fabricate, fit and maintain artificial limbs."]],
  refs:[{b:"osullivan",ch:"Clinical decision making and the rehabilitation team"},{c:"Frontera WR (ed). DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. Chapter: The rehabilitation team."},{c:"Langhorne P et al. Organised inpatient (stroke unit) care for stroke. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews.", q:"organised inpatient stroke unit care Cochrane"}],
  keywords:"rehabilitation team multidisciplinary interdisciplinary transdisciplinary roles physiotherapist occupational therapist",
  mcq:[["A team model where one member delivers most care while others consult is:",["Multidisciplinary","Interdisciplinary","Transdisciplinary","Unidisciplinary"],2,"Transdisciplinary teams share roles across disciplines."],["ADL training and adaptive devices are mainly the role of the:",["Occupational therapist","Prosthetist","Social worker","Speech therapist"],0,"OT focuses on daily activities and adaptations."]]
}
