{
  id:"co-endurance-strengthening", title:"Increasing endurance and strengthening in rehabilitation", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"General", stages:"steps",
  aliases:["endurance training","muscular endurance","cardiovascular endurance","strength training rehabilitation","aerobic training disabled","progressive resistance exercise"],
  simple:"Strength is how much force a muscle can produce once; endurance is how long a muscle or the whole body can keep working. Disabled and bedridden people lose both quickly. Rehabilitation uses heavy loads with few repetitions to build strength and lighter, longer or repeated work to build endurance, progressing step by step.",
  flow:["Assess strength (MMT, 1RM) and endurance (6MWT, HR)","Set goal: strength, muscle endurance or aerobic capacity","Choose mode: weights, bands, cycle, walking, arm ergometer","Dose with FITT and overload","Monitor HR, RPE, BP and fatigue","Progress gradually, maintain with home programme"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definitions", p:["Muscle strength: maximum force a muscle or group can generate in one effort.","Muscular endurance: ability to perform repeated contractions or hold a contraction against submaximal load over time.","Cardiovascular (aerobic) endurance: ability of heart, lungs and muscles to sustain whole-body activity; measured by VO2 max.","Power: work per unit time (force times velocity)."]},
    {h:"Principles", p:["Overload: load must exceed what the tissue is used to.","Specificity (SAID): gains match the type, speed, range and position of training.","Progression, individualisation, reversibility (detraining after about 2 weeks of inactivity).","Adequate warm-up, cool down and rest between sessions."]},
    {h:"Strengthening methods and dosage", p:["Grade-based progression: grade 1-2 facilitation and assisted exercise, grade 3 against gravity, grade 4-5 resisted.","Progressive resistance exercise: DeLorme (3 sets of 10 at 50, 75, 100 percent of 10RM), Oxford (reverse), DAPRE.","ACSM: 60-80 percent of 1RM, 8-12 repetitions, 2-3 sets, 2-3 days a week for each major muscle group; older or frail adults may start at 40-50 percent 1RM.","Modes: isometric (in pain or immobilisation), isotonic (free weights, bands, pulleys), isokinetic, closed chain functional exercise, PNF, NMES for very weak muscles."]},
    {h:"Endurance training methods and dosage", p:["Muscle endurance: low load (30-50 percent 1RM), 15-25 repetitions, multiple sets, short rest; circuit training.","Aerobic endurance: walking, cycling, arm ergometry, swimming, wheelchair propulsion; 3-5 days a week, moderate intensity (40-60 percent heart rate reserve or RPE 11-14), 20-60 minutes, can be split into 10-minute bouts.","Interval training for deconditioned patients: short work periods alternated with rest.","Karvonen formula: target HR = resting HR + intensity x (max HR - resting HR)."]},
    {h:"Application in disability", p:["SCI: arm ergometry, wheelchair training, FES cycling.","Stroke: treadmill with body-weight support, task-specific strength training.","Cardiac and respiratory patients: monitored aerobic programmes.","Elderly and post-fracture: progressive resistance and balance exercise to prevent falls.","Neuromuscular disease: avoid exhaustive exercise; submaximal training with rest."]},
    {h:"Precautions and conclusion", p:["Avoid Valsalva (breathe out during effort); monitor BP in hypertensives; stop with chest pain, dizziness, unusual breathlessness.","Avoid overwork weakness in LMN disorders (polio, GBS, myopathy).","Combining strength and endurance training improves function, independence and quality of life in most disabled people."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a continuum line with high load and low repetitions (strength) at one end and low load and high repetitions (endurance) at the other. Below it write the Karvonen formula with an example calculation.",
  evidence:[["Progressive resistance training in older adults","Improves strength and physical function","High"],["Aerobic training after stroke","Improves walking speed and endurance","High"],["Exercise training in COPD","Improves exercise capacity and quality of life","High"]],
  evidenceSource:"ACSM Guidelines for Exercise Testing and Prescription; Cochrane reviews of resistance and aerobic training",
  outcomes:[["1RM or MMT","Strength"],["6-minute walk test","Functional endurance"],["Borg RPE","Exertion"],["VO2 max or peak","Aerobic capacity"]],
  recent:["High-intensity interval training is being studied and used carefully in cardiac and neurological rehabilitation."],
  mnemonic:{word:"FITT", items:[["F","Frequency"],["I","Intensity"],["T","Time"],["T","Type"]], extra:"Strength: heavy, few. Endurance: light, many."},
  example:"A 62-year-old retired bank officer in Madurai is weak after 3 weeks in hospital. He starts sit-to-stand and band exercises at 2 sets of 10 and walks 10 minutes twice daily at RPE 12, progressing to 30 minutes over 6 weeks.",
  flags:["Chest pain, arrhythmia or abnormal BP response during exercise","Valsalva during heavy lifting in cardiac patients","Overwork weakness in LMN disease","Pain lasting more than 2 hours after exercise"],
  viva:[["What is SAID principle?","Specific adaptation to imposed demands: the body adapts specifically to the training given."],["Give the Karvonen formula.","Target HR = resting HR + intensity x (maximum HR - resting HR)."],["Dosage of DeLorme technique?","3 sets of 10 repetitions at 50, 75 and 100 percent of 10RM."]],
  refs:[{b:"acsm",ch:"Exercise prescription"},{b:"kisner",ch:"Resistance exercise and aerobic exercise"},{b:"houglum",ch:"Muscle strength and endurance"}],
  keywords:"strength endurance aerobic training PRE DeLorme Karvonen FITT overload rehabilitation",
  mcq:[["For building muscular endurance, the best prescription is:",["High load, 3-5 reps","Low load, 15-25 reps","Maximal isometric once","1RM daily"],1,"Endurance needs many repetitions with light loads."],["Overwork weakness is a particular risk in:",["Healthy athletes","Post-polio and LMN disorders","Knee OA","Ankle sprain"],1,"Partly denervated muscles fatigue and may lose strength with excessive work."]]
}
