{
  id:"co-tendon-transfer-reeducation", title:"Muscle re-education after tendon transfer", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"Upper and lower limb", stages:"steps",
  aliases:["tendon transfer rehabilitation","re-education of transferred muscle","post tendon transfer physiotherapy","opponensplasty rehab","tibialis posterior transfer rehab","Zancolli lasso rehab"],
  simple:"In a tendon transfer, the surgeon moves a working muscle's tendon to do the job of a paralysed one. The brain still thinks of the muscle in its old job, so the patient must learn to use it for the new movement. Physiotherapy protects the repair first, then teaches the new action step by step until it becomes automatic.",
  flow:["Pre-operative strengthening and full passive range","Surgery: donor tendon rerouted to new insertion","Immobilise 3-6 weeks to protect the junction","Activate donor using its old action plus new movement","Isolate new action, add resistance and function","Automatic use in daily tasks and gait"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Introduction", p:["Tendon transfer: detaching a functioning muscle-tendon unit and reattaching it to restore lost function (examples: polio, leprosy, peripheral nerve injury, brachial plexus, tendon rupture, CP).","Success depends on surgical principles (donor grade 4-5, supple joint, synergy, straight line of pull, one function per transfer) and on re-education.","Expect loss of about one MRC grade in the donor after transfer."]},
    {h:"Pre-operative phase", p:["Achieve full passive ROM and correct contractures (stretching, serial splints).","Strengthen donor muscle to maximum; teach isolated contraction of the donor.","Explain the new movement; practise imagery; assess sensation and skin."]},
    {h:"Immobilisation phase (about 0-3 to 6 weeks)", p:["Cast or splint in a position that keeps the transfer slack (e.g. wrist and fingers extended after radial palsy transfer, ankle dorsiflexed after tibialis posterior transfer).","Elevation, active exercise of free joints, isometric of uninvolved muscles, no stretch of the transfer.","Some protocols begin early protected active motion with modern strong weaves; follow the surgeon."]},
    {h:"Re-education phase (about 3-6 to 12 weeks)", p:["Start in gravity-eliminated position with short arc active assisted movement.","Ask the patient to perform the donor's original action while attempting the new action (e.g. 'flex wrist' to activate FCU now extending fingers; 'invert foot' to activate tibialis posterior now dorsiflexing).","Use facilitation: tapping, approximation, light resistance, PNF patterns, EMG biofeedback, NMES over the donor muscle, mirror and visual feedback.","Avoid stretching the transfer beyond neutral and avoid resisted antagonist movement until about 8-12 weeks.","Gradually remove the cue until the new movement is performed independently."]},
    {h:"Strengthening and functional phase (after about 8-12 weeks)", p:["Progressive resisted exercise, endurance and speed.","Task-specific training: grasp and release, pinch, writing, eating for upper limb; heel strike, gait training with AFO weaned gradually for foot drop.","Night splint to protect for some months; home programme; sensory re-education where needed.","Re-assess with MMT, ROM, grip and pinch, gait analysis."]},
    {h:"Complications and conclusion", p:["Adhesions, rupture or stretching of transfer, imbalance or new deformity, failure to learn new action.","Good re-education converts a surgically moved muscle into a useful, automatic function."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a foot with tibialis posterior tendon passing through the interosseous membrane to the dorsum (lateral cuneiform). Add a timeline below: pre-op, 0-6 weeks immobilise, 6-12 weeks re-educate, 12+ weeks strengthen and function.",
  evidence:[["EMG biofeedback in tendon transfer re-education","May speed learning of new action","Low"],["Early active motion after upper limb tendon transfer","Function similar or faster than prolonged immobilisation in selected cases","Low to moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Hand therapy and orthopaedic texts; small trials of early mobilisation after tendon transfer",
  outcomes:[["MMT of transferred muscle","Strength"],["Active ROM","Excursion of transfer"],["Grip and pinch strength","Hand function"],["Gait analysis","Foot clearance after transfer"]],
  recent:["Stronger tendon weave techniques allow some surgeons to start early active motion within days after transfer."],
  mnemonic:{word:"PIRF", items:[["P","Prepare: ROM and donor strength"],["I","Immobilise slack"],["R","Re-educate using old action"],["F","Function and strength"]], extra:"Donor must be grade 4 or more."},
  example:"A 25-year-old man in Tirunelveli with leprosy foot drop has a tibialis posterior transfer. After 6 weeks in a cast he practises 'turning the foot in' while lifting it up, with biofeedback, and by 12 weeks walks without his AFO on level ground.",
  flags:["Sudden loss of the new movement (rupture)","Passive stretching of the transfer in early weeks","Development of new deformity from imbalance","Skin breakdown under cast or splint in anaesthetic limbs"],
  viva:[["Why is a donor muscle of grade 4 or more needed?","Because a transferred muscle usually loses about one grade of power."],["How do you activate a transferred muscle first?","Ask the patient to perform its original action while attempting the new movement."],["When is resisted exercise usually begun?","Around 8-12 weeks, when the tendon junction has healed."]],
  refs:[{b:"cashOrtho",ch:"Tendon transfers"},{c:"Maheshwari J, Mhaskar VA. Essential Orthopaedics. Chapter: Principles of tendon transfer."},{b:"kisner",ch:"Wrist and hand; ankle and foot surgery"}],
  keywords:"tendon transfer re-education muscle re-education biofeedback donor muscle tibialis posterior opponensplasty",
  mcq:[["In the first stage of re-education after tibialis posterior transfer, the patient is asked to:",["Plantarflex forcefully","Invert the foot while dorsiflexing","Evert the foot","Stretch the calf"],1,"Using the donor's original action helps activate the transferred muscle."],["During the immobilisation phase, the transfer is kept:",["On maximum stretch","Slack","Under resistance","Mobilised passively to end range"],1,"A slack position protects the healing tendon junction."]]
}
