{
  id:"co-communication-disorders", title:"Communication problems: classification and treatment (aphasia, dysarthria, apraxia of speech, hearing loss)", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"Head and neck", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["communication disorders","aphasia","dysarthria","speech disorders","Broca aphasia","Wernicke aphasia","augmentative and alternative communication"],
  simple:"Communication means understanding and expressing messages. After a stroke, head injury or in cerebral palsy, a person may lose language (aphasia), have slurred speech from weak muscles (dysarthria), cannot plan speech movements (apraxia), or may not hear well. A speech therapist leads treatment, but every team member must use simple strategies so the patient can take part in rehab.",
  flow:["Stroke, TBI, CP, Parkinson, hearing loss, laryngeal disease","Damage to language centres, speech muscles or hearing","Difficulty understanding, speaking, reading or writing","Screen, then speech therapist assessment","Speech-language therapy, AAC, hearing aids","Team strategies, family training, social participation"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Classification", p:["Language disorders: aphasia (acquired loss of language due to brain damage), developmental language disorder in children.","Speech disorders: dysarthria (motor speech weakness or incoordination), apraxia of speech (motor planning), stuttering, voice disorders (dysphonia, aphonia, laryngectomy).","Hearing disorders: conductive, sensorineural, mixed hearing loss; deaf-mutism.","Cognitive-communication disorders: after TBI or dementia (poor attention, memory, pragmatics)."]},
    {h:"Aphasia types", p:["Broca (expressive, non-fluent): left inferior frontal lesion; slow effortful speech, good comprehension, often right hemiplegia.","Wernicke (receptive, fluent): left superior temporal lesion; fluent meaningless speech, poor comprehension, unaware of errors.","Global: large left MCA lesion; both expression and comprehension lost.","Conduction (poor repetition), anomic (word-finding difficulty), transcortical types."]},
    {h:"Dysarthria and apraxia", p:["Dysarthria types: flaccid (LMN, bulbar palsy), spastic (UMN, pseudobulbar), ataxic (cerebellar, scanning speech), hypokinetic (Parkinson, soft monotonous), hyperkinetic (chorea, dystonia), mixed (MND, MS).","Apraxia of speech: inconsistent errors, groping for sounds, with intact muscles."]},
    {h:"Treatment", p:["Speech-language therapy: naming, comprehension tasks, melodic intonation therapy (Broca), constraint-induced aphasia therapy, script training.","Dysarthria: oromotor exercises, breathing and phonation control, pacing boards, LSVT LOUD for Parkinson.","AAC: communication boards, picture cards, writing pads, electronic devices and apps, gestures.","Hearing loss: hearing aids, cochlear implants, sign language, lip reading.","Treat dysphagia often present with dysarthria."]},
    {h:"Role of physiotherapist and team", p:["Speak slowly, use short sentences and gestures, face the patient, reduce noise, allow time to respond.","Use yes or no questions and demonstration to give exercise instructions; check understanding.","Positioning and trunk control for breathing and voice; respiratory muscle training; head and neck posture.","Encourage communication during therapy; involve family; refer early to speech therapist."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Classifying the problem as language, speech, hearing or cognitive guides treatment. Team-wide communication strategies let the patient participate fully in rehabilitation."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw the left side of the brain and mark Broca area (frontal) and Wernicke area (temporal) joined by the arcuate fasciculus. Write the aphasia type that follows damage at each site.",
  evidence:[["Speech and language therapy after stroke aphasia","Improves functional communication, especially at high intensity","Moderate"],["LSVT LOUD in Parkinson's disease","Improves voice loudness","Low to moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane review of speech and language therapy for aphasia after stroke (Brady et al)",
  outcomes:[["Western Aphasia Battery","Type and severity of aphasia"],["Frenchay Dysarthria Assessment","Dysarthria profile"],["Audiometry","Hearing level"],["Functional communication measures","Everyday communication"]],
  recent:["Tablet-based apps and computer therapy programmes now supplement speech therapy for aphasia in low-resource settings."],
  mnemonic:{word:"BW", items:[["B","Broca: Broken speech, understands"],["W","Wernicke: Word salad, doesn't understand"]], extra:"Dysarthria = muscle problem; aphasia = language problem."},
  example:"A 60-year-old auto driver in Salem has a left MCA stroke with right hemiplegia and halting speech but follows commands. The physio uses demonstration and short sentences while the speech therapist starts melodic intonation therapy.",
  flags:["Sudden new speech difficulty (possible new stroke, emergency)","Dysarthria with cough on swallowing (aspiration risk)","Assuming aphasic patients cannot understand or have low intelligence","Hearing loss mistaken for confusion"],
  viva:[["Difference between aphasia and dysarthria?","Aphasia is a language disorder; dysarthria is a motor speech disorder from muscle weakness or incoordination."],["Where is Broca area?","Left inferior frontal gyrus."],["Name a communication aid.","Picture communication board or electronic AAC device."]],
  refs:[{b:"osullivan",ch:"Cognitive and perceptual dysfunction and stroke"},{c:"Brady MC et al. Speech and language therapy for aphasia following stroke. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2016.", q:"Brady speech and language therapy aphasia stroke Cochrane"},{c:"Frontera WR (ed). DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. Chapter: Communication disorders."}],
  keywords:"communication disorders aphasia dysarthria apraxia hearing loss AAC Broca Wernicke speech therapy",
  mcq:[["Fluent speech with poor comprehension is typical of:",["Broca aphasia","Wernicke aphasia","Flaccid dysarthria","Global aphasia"],1,"Wernicke lesion spares fluency but impairs comprehension."],["Scanning speech is a feature of:",["Hypokinetic dysarthria","Ataxic dysarthria","Spastic dysarthria","Apraxia of speech"],1,"Cerebellar lesions cause ataxic, scanning speech."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-behavioural-problems", title:"Behavioural problems in the disabled", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"General", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["behavioural problems disability","psychological reactions to disability","depression after disability","adjustment to disability","stages of adjustment","behaviour modification rehabilitation"],
  simple:"Becoming disabled is a huge shock, so many people go through denial, anger, sadness and slowly acceptance. Brain injury itself can cause irritability, impulsiveness or loss of motivation. These behaviours can slow rehab, so the team must understand the cause, stay calm, reward good behaviour and involve psychologists and family.",
  flow:["Sudden loss, brain damage, pain, dependence, stigma","Emotional reaction and altered behaviour","Denial, anger, depression, aggression, dependence","Observe, screen mood and cognition, find triggers","Counselling, behaviour therapy, drugs if needed","Consistent team approach, family support, participation"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Causes", p:["Psychological reaction to loss of function, body image, job and role.","Organic brain damage: TBI (frontal lobe), stroke (especially right hemisphere neglect and denial), dementia, intellectual disability, autism.","Pain, fatigue, sleep disturbance, drugs, infection or delirium.","Social factors: poverty, stigma, family conflict, over-protection, loss of income."]},
    {h:"Common behavioural problems", p:["Stages of adjustment (similar to grief): shock, denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance; patients may move back and forth.","Depression and anxiety (common after stroke, SCI, amputation), suicidal thoughts.","Aggression, irritability, disinhibition, impulsivity, emotional lability (pathological crying or laughing).","Apathy and poor motivation, excessive dependence (learned helplessness), secondary gain, non-compliance, denial of disability.","In children: tantrums, hyperactivity, self-injury, attention problems."]},
    {h:"Assessment", p:["Observe behaviour in different settings; ABC analysis (antecedent, behaviour, consequence).","Screen mood (PHQ-9, HADS, Geriatric Depression Scale) and cognition (MMSE).","Rule out medical causes: pain, infection, medication, delirium.","Take history from family about pre-injury personality."]},
    {h:"Management", p:["Counselling and psychotherapy: supportive counselling, cognitive behavioural therapy, peer support from others with similar disability.","Behaviour modification: positive reinforcement of desired behaviour, token economy, shaping, ignoring attention-seeking behaviour, time-out, clear routines.","Environmental management: calm, structured, low-stimulus setting for TBI agitation; consistent staff.","Medication by psychiatrist: antidepressants, anxiolytics, mood stabilisers when needed.","Family education and involvement; vocational and recreational activities to restore purpose."]},
    {h:"Role of physiotherapist", p:["Build rapport and trust; explain goals and give choices to increase control.","Set small achievable goals to provide success; praise effort.","Be consistent, calm and firm with agitated patients; avoid arguing; ensure safety.","Recognise depression and refer; exercise itself improves mood.","Group therapy and sports for socialisation."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Behaviour problems are common and understandable responses to disability or brain injury. A consistent, empathetic team approach with behaviour therapy improves participation and rehab outcome."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a curve of the stages of adjustment (shock, denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance) going down then up over time. Below it draw a box diagram for ABC analysis: Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence.",
  evidence:[["Psychological therapy for depression after stroke","Modest improvement in mood","Moderate"],["Exercise for depression","Reduces depressive symptoms","Moderate"],["Behaviour management for agitation after TBI","Supported by clinical consensus; trials limited","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane reviews on interventions for post-stroke depression and exercise for depression",
  outcomes:[["PHQ-9 or HADS","Depression and anxiety"],["Agitated Behaviour Scale","TBI agitation"],["Participation in therapy","Engagement"],["Quality of life scales","Wellbeing"]],
  recent:["Peer mentoring by trained people with spinal cord injury or amputation is increasingly built into rehab programmes."],
  mnemonic:{word:"ABC", items:[["A","Antecedent: what triggers it"],["B","Behaviour: what happens"],["C","Consequence: what follows and maintains it"]], extra:"Reward good behaviour, do not reward unwanted behaviour."},
  example:"A 21-year-old engineering student in Coimbatore with paraplegia after a bike accident refuses therapy and shouts at staff. The team recognises anger and depression, arranges counselling and a peer visit from a wheelchair athlete, and sets small daily goals he can achieve.",
  flags:["Suicidal thoughts or plans","Sudden behaviour change (delirium, infection, new bleed)","Aggression risking patient or staff safety","Severe depression stopping all participation"],
  viva:[["What is learned helplessness?","Passive dependence learned after repeated loss of control, even when the person could do more."],["What is token economy?","A reinforcement system where tokens earned for desired behaviour are exchanged for rewards."],["Name a screening tool for depression.","PHQ-9 or HADS."]],
  refs:[{b:"osullivan",ch:"Psychosocial issues in physical rehabilitation"},{c:"Frontera WR (ed). DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. Chapter: Psychological aspects of rehabilitation."},{b:"umphred",ch:"Psychosocial aspects and adjustment"}],
  keywords:"behavioural problems disability depression adjustment stages behaviour modification ABC token economy",
  mcq:[["In behaviour modification, the best response to attention-seeking unwanted behaviour is usually:",["Punish loudly","Planned ignoring and rewarding desired behaviour","Give extra attention","Stop therapy permanently"],1,"Withdrawing attention removes the reinforcer, while praise rewards wanted behaviour."],["Pathological crying and laughing after brain injury is called:",["Apathy","Emotional lability","Denial","Secondary gain"],1,"Emotional lability (pseudobulbar affect) follows brain damage."]]
}
,
{
  id:"co-pain-theories-modalities", title:"Theories of pain and modality-based management of pain", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"General", stages:"steps",
  aliases:["theories of pain","gate control theory","pain modulation","specificity theory","pattern theory","endogenous opioid","pain management modalities"],
  simple:"Pain is the brain's alarm system, not just a signal from damaged tissue. Older theories saw pain as a simple wire from body to brain; the gate control theory showed that touch and the brain itself can close a 'gate' in the spinal cord. Physiotherapy modalities like TENS, heat, cold and exercise work partly by closing this gate or releasing the body's own painkillers.",
  flow:["Nociceptors detect tissue damage","A-delta and C fibres carry signals to dorsal horn","Gate in substantia gelatinosa modulates transmission","Brain interprets and modulates pain (descending control)","Choose modality to act on gate or opioid system","Combine with education and active exercise"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definition and types of pain", p:["IASP (2020): an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with, or resembling that associated with, actual or potential tissue damage.","Acute versus chronic (over 3 months); nociceptive, neuropathic and nociplastic pain.","Pain pathway: nociceptor, A-delta (fast, sharp) and C fibres (slow, dull) to dorsal horn, spinothalamic tract, thalamus, somatosensory cortex and limbic areas."]},
    {h:"Theories of pain", p:["Specificity theory (Descartes, von Frey): specific pain receptors and a dedicated pathway; cannot explain phantom pain or modulation.","Pattern (intensity) theory (Goldscheider): pain from excessive stimulation of any receptor or a pattern of impulses.","Gate control theory (Melzack and Wall, 1965): substantia gelatinosa cells in the dorsal horn act as a gate; large A-beta (touch) fibre input closes the gate, small A-delta and C fibre input opens it; descending signals from the brain also modulate the gate.","Endogenous opioid (descending inhibition) theory: periaqueductal grey and raphe nuclei release enkephalins, endorphins and serotonin that inhibit dorsal horn transmission.","Neuromatrix theory (Melzack): pain is an output of a widely distributed brain network influenced by sensory, emotional and cognitive inputs; central sensitisation explains chronic pain."]},
    {h:"Modality-based management", p:["TENS: conventional (high frequency 80-150 Hz, 50-100 microseconds, comfortable tingling, gate control) and acupuncture-like (2-4 Hz, 150-250 microseconds, strong visible contraction, endorphin release); 20-60 minutes.","IFT: two medium frequency currents (about 4000 Hz) producing beat frequency 80-150 Hz for pain relief.","Heat (hot packs, IRR, SWD, wax): reduces muscle spasm, increases blood flow and pain threshold; 15-20 minutes.","Cold (ice packs 10-20 minutes): slows nerve conduction and reduces inflammation and spasm.","Ultrasound, laser: limited evidence for pain; massage and manual therapy stimulate A-beta fibres; exercise induces hypoalgesia."]},
    {h:"Non-modality strategies", p:["Pain neuroscience education, graded activity and graded exposure.","Relaxation, breathing, mindfulness, cognitive behavioural approaches.","Medication and interventional pain procedures by physicians; multidisciplinary pain programmes for chronic pain."]},
    {h:"Contraindications and precautions", p:["TENS: demand pacemaker, over the carotid sinus, anterior neck, eyes, pregnant uterus, broken skin, epilepsy (caution), undiagnosed pain.","Heat: impaired sensation, acute inflammation, bleeding, malignancy, DVT; cold: cold hypersensitivity, Raynaud, impaired circulation.","Passive modalities alone can encourage dependence in chronic pain; combine with active care."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["Gate control and descending inhibition explain how modalities relieve pain, while the neuromatrix explains chronic pain. Modalities are useful short-term adjuncts to education and exercise."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw the gate control diagram: large fibre (L) and small fibre (S) entering the dorsal horn, both synapsing on a T (transmission) cell, with an SG (substantia gelatinosa) cell that is excited by L (closing gate) and inhibited by S (opening gate), plus a descending arrow from the brain.",
  evidence:[["TENS for acute and chronic pain","Some pain reduction; evidence quality low to moderate","Low to moderate"],["Pain neuroscience education with exercise in chronic pain","Reduces pain and disability","Moderate"],["Exercise-induced hypoalgesia","Consistent short-term pain reduction in healthy people; variable in chronic pain","Moderate"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane overviews of TENS and systematic reviews of pain neuroscience education",
  outcomes:[["VAS or NPRS","Pain intensity"],["McGill Pain Questionnaire","Quality of pain"],["Pressure pain threshold","Sensitivity"],["Disability index (ODI, NDI)","Pain-related function"]],
  recent:["IASP revised the definition of pain in 2020 and recognises nociplastic pain as a third mechanism."],
  mnemonic:{word:"SPGEN", items:[["S","Specificity"],["P","Pattern"],["G","Gate control"],["E","Endogenous opioid"],["N","Neuromatrix"]], extra:"Big fibres close the gate."},
  example:"A 35-year-old tailor in Kanchipuram with acute neck spasm gets hot packs and conventional TENS at 100 Hz for 20 minutes, which reduces pain enough to start active neck exercises and posture correction.",
  flags:["Pain with fever, weight loss or night pain (serious pathology)","TENS near a demand pacemaker","Heat over anaesthetic skin","Over-reliance on passive modalities in chronic pain"],
  viva:[["Who proposed the gate control theory?","Melzack and Wall in 1965."],["Which fibres close the gate?","Large diameter A-beta fibres."],["Parameters of acupuncture-like TENS?","Low frequency 2-4 Hz, long pulse 150-250 microseconds, strong intensity."]],
  refs:[{c:"Melzack R, Wall PD. Pain mechanisms: a new theory. Science 1965.", q:"Melzack Wall pain mechanisms new theory 1965"},{b:"cameron",ch:"Pain and physical agents"},{b:"explainPain",ch:"Pain biology"}],
  keywords:"pain theories gate control specificity pattern endogenous opioid neuromatrix TENS modality management",
  mcq:[["According to gate control theory, the gate is located in the:",["Thalamus","Substantia gelatinosa of dorsal horn","Motor cortex","Dorsal root ganglion"],1,"SG cells in the dorsal horn modulate transmission."],["Conventional TENS mainly works by:",["Endorphin release only","Stimulating large A-beta fibres to close the gate","Heating tissue","Blocking motor nerves"],1,"High frequency, low intensity TENS stimulates A-beta fibres."]]
}
