{
  id:"co-myofascial-pain", title:"Myofascial pain syndrome and trigger points", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"General", stages:"012344",
  aliases:["myofascial pain syndrome","trigger points","MPS","taut band","referred pain trigger point","dry needling","ischaemic compression"],
  simple:"Myofascial pain comes from small, very tender knots called trigger points inside a tight band of muscle. Pressing a trigger point reproduces the patient's pain, often in a distant area. Overuse, bad posture and stress keep them active. Treatment releases the knot with pressure, stretching or needling and then fixes the habits that caused it.",
  flow:["Overload, poor posture, trauma, stress, repetitive work","Local energy crisis forms taut band and trigger point","Regional pain, referred pain, stiffness, weakness","Palpate taut band, tender nodule, local twitch response","Pressure release, spray and stretch, dry needling, modalities","Correct posture and ergonomics, home stretching"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definition", p:["Regional muscle pain caused by myofascial trigger points (MTrPs): hyperirritable spots within a palpable taut band of skeletal muscle that are painful on compression and give characteristic referred pain, motor dysfunction and autonomic signs.","Active trigger point: causes spontaneous pain; latent trigger point: tender only on palpation but causes stiffness and weakness."]},
    {h:"Causes and pathophysiology", p:["Acute overload, repetitive microtrauma, sustained postures (computer work, mobile phone use), poor ergonomics, cold, psychological stress, sleep problems, nutritional deficiency (vitamin D, B12, iron), hypothyroidism.","Integrated hypothesis: excess acetylcholine at the motor end plate, sustained sarcomere contraction, local ischaemia and energy crisis, release of sensitising substances causing pain.","Central sensitisation may maintain chronic MPS; mechanisms still debated."]},
    {h:"Clinical features and diagnosis", p:["Dull, aching regional pain with referred pattern (upper trapezius to temple and neck, infraspinatus to front of shoulder, gluteus minimus down the leg mimicking sciatica).","Taut band, exquisite spot tenderness, jump sign, pain recognition, local twitch response on snapping palpation.","Restricted stretch range, weakness without atrophy; no neurological deficit.","Diagnosis is clinical; investigations only to exclude other causes. Differentiate from fibromyalgia (widespread pain, multiple tender points, fatigue, sleep problems)."]},
    {h:"Physiotherapy management", p:["Ischaemic compression (trigger point pressure release): sustained pressure 30-90 seconds until tension eases, repeat 3-5 times.","Spray and stretch (vapocoolant spray in parallel sweeps followed by passive stretch), muscle energy techniques, myofascial release, deep stroking massage.","Dry needling (by trained therapists where permitted) or medical trigger point injection with local anaesthetic.","Moist heat, ultrasound (1 MHz, 1-1.5 W/cm2, 5 minutes) and TENS as adjuncts; evidence for passive modalities is weak.","Home stretching, posture and ergonomic correction, strengthening and endurance training, stress management and sleep hygiene."]},
    {h:"Precautions", p:["Needling: avoid over lungs (pneumothorax), in bleeding disorders, infection, needle phobia, first trimester pregnancy.","Exclude serious pathology (tumour, infection, radiculopathy) before treating."]},
    {h:"Conclusion", p:["MPS is common and treatable. Release of the trigger point gives short-term relief, but lasting results need correction of perpetuating factors and active exercise."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a muscle with a taut band running through it and a star for the trigger point. Draw the upper trapezius trigger point with dotted referral to the side of the head and neck.",
  evidence:[["Dry needling for myofascial pain","Short-term pain reduction compared with sham or no treatment","Low to moderate"],["Ischaemic compression","Short-term pain relief","Low"],["Stretching and exercise programmes","Help reduce recurrence","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"Systematic reviews of dry needling and manual therapy for myofascial trigger points; Travell and Simons",
  outcomes:[["Pressure pain threshold (algometer)","Trigger point sensitivity"],["NPRS","Pain intensity"],["ROM","Muscle extensibility"],["Neck Disability Index","Function"]],
  recent:["Ultrasound elastography is being explored to visualise trigger points, but diagnosis remains clinical."],
  mnemonic:{word:"TRAP", items:[["T","Taut band"],["R","Referred pain"],["A","Abnormal twitch (local twitch response)"],["P","Point tenderness and Pain recognition"]], extra:"Fix posture or it comes back."},
  example:"A 26-year-old software engineer in Chennai has headaches and neck pain after long hours on his laptop. A tender knot in the upper trapezius reproduces his temple pain; ischaemic compression, stretching and raising his screen bring relief.",
  flags:["Night pain, weight loss or fever","Neurological deficit (suggests radiculopathy)","Widespread pain with fatigue (consider fibromyalgia)","Needling over the thorax without training"],
  viva:[["What is a local twitch response?","A brief contraction of the taut band fibres when the trigger point is snapped or needled."],["Difference between active and latent trigger point?","Active causes spontaneous pain; latent is painful only when pressed."],["What is spray and stretch?","Vapocoolant spray applied over the muscle and referral zone followed by passive stretch."]],
  refs:[{b:"travell",ch:"General overview of myofascial pain"},{b:"cameron",ch:"Pain management"},{c:"Simons DG. Review of enigmatic MTrPs as a common cause of enigmatic musculoskeletal pain and dysfunction. J Electromyogr Kinesiol 2004.", q:"Simons enigmatic MTrPs musculoskeletal pain 2004"}],
  keywords:"myofascial pain syndrome trigger point taut band referred pain ischaemic compression dry needling spray and stretch",
  mcq:[["A hyperirritable spot in a taut band of muscle is a:",["Tender point of fibromyalgia","Myofascial trigger point","Ganglion","Bursa"],1,"This is the definition of a trigger point."],["Ischaemic compression is applied for about:",["1-2 seconds","30-90 seconds","10 minutes","30 minutes"],1,"Sustained pressure until the tissue softens, usually under 90 seconds."]]
}
