{
  id:"co-architectural-barriers", title:"Architectural barriers and environmental modifications for RA, CVA and SCI", subject:"Rehabilitation", region:"General", stages:"steps",
  aliases:["architectural barriers","home modification","barrier free design","accessibility ramp gradient","environmental modification","wheelchair accessibility"],
  simple:"A person may be able to move well in the therapy room but still be stuck at home because of steps, narrow doors or an Indian toilet. Architectural barriers are such obstacles in buildings and surroundings. Modifying homes, workplaces and public places lets people with arthritis, stroke or spinal cord injury live independently.",
  flow:["Identify patient's mobility level and needs","Home and workplace visit: entrance, rooms, toilet, kitchen","Find barriers: steps, width, height, surfaces, reach","Plan modifications to standards","Train patient and family in modified environment","Review safety and advocate for public access"],
  exam:[
    {h:"Definition and types", p:["Architectural barriers: physical features of buildings and environment that prevent access or safe use by disabled persons.","External: kerbs, steps, uneven roads, lack of ramps, inaccessible transport and parking.","Internal: narrow doors, thresholds, stairs, small toilets, high switches and shelves, slippery floors, Indian-style squat toilets, low beds.","Universal design: environments usable by all people without adaptation; India follows the Harmonised Guidelines and Standards for Universal Accessibility (2021) and the Accessible India Campaign."]},
    {h:"General standards (wheelchair users)", p:["Ramp gradient 1:12 (about 8 percent) or gentler, minimum width about 1200 mm, level landing every 9 m and at top and bottom, handrails on both sides at about 760-900 mm, non-slip surface, kerbs on edges.","Door clear width at least 900 mm (minimum 800 mm), lever handles, no raised thresholds; turning space 1500 mm diameter.","Accessible toilet with western WC at 450-480 mm seat height, horizontal grab bars beside the WC, wash basin with knee space, mirror tilted.","Switches 800-1100 mm high; tables 750-800 mm with knee clearance; firm, non-slip flooring; good lighting."]},
    {h:"Modifications for rheumatoid arthritis", p:["Reduce load on small joints: lever taps and door handles, built-up handles, electric switches within reach, lightweight utensils.","Raised toilet seat, raised chair and bed height to ease sit-to-stand; grab bars and handrails.","Kitchen at elbow height, frequently used items between shoulder and hip height, trolley for carrying, avoid stooping and low shelves.","Avoid stairs where possible (ground floor bedroom), non-slip mats, long-handled reachers and sock aids."]},
    {h:"Modifications for CVA (hemiplegia)", p:["Handrails on the unaffected side for stairs (both sides ideal), grab bars in toilet and bath, shower chair.","Bed placed so the patient gets out towards the stronger side; furniture arranged to encourage attention to the affected side (neglect) while keeping walkways clear.","Remove loose rugs and clutter, good lighting, night light; one-handed kitchen aids (spike board, non-slip mat).","Wheelchair or walker access to essential rooms."]},
    {h:"Modifications for SCI", p:["Full wheelchair accessibility: ramp or lift, wide doors, turning space, roll-in shower with shower chair, accessible WC with transfer space.","Lower counters and switches, side-opening ovens, knee clearance under sinks (insulate hot pipes to protect insensate legs).","Tetraplegia: environmental control units, voice-controlled devices, hoists, electric bed and pressure-relief mattress.","Accessible vehicle and parking; workplace adaptations."]},
    {h:"Role of physiotherapist and conclusion", p:["Home visit and assessment, measurements, recommendations, training in transfers and mobility in the real environment.","Advocacy for accessible public buildings under the RPwD Act 2016.","Removing barriers often improves independence more than additional therapy."]}
  ],
  tip:"Draw a side view of a ramp marking the 1:12 gradient (1 unit rise for 12 units length), handrails and landing. Next to it draw a toilet plan with a 1500 mm turning circle, WC with grab bars and 900 mm door.",
  evidence:[["Home hazard modification by occupational therapists in high-risk older people","Reduces falls","Moderate"],["Home modifications after SCI and stroke","Improve independence in observational studies","Low"]],
  evidenceSource:"Cochrane review of falls prevention interventions in community-dwelling older people; accessibility standards",
  outcomes:[["Home Falls and Accidents Screening Tool (HOME FAST)","Home hazards"],["Barthel Index or FIM","Independence"],["Housing Enabler","Person-environment fit"],["Community participation measures","Access to society"]],
  recent:["India's Harmonised Guidelines and Standards for Universal Accessibility (2021) update building access norms under the Accessible India Campaign."],
  mnemonic:{word:"RDT", items:[["R","Ramp 1:12"],["D","Door 900 mm"],["T","Turning circle 1500 mm"]], extra:"Western WC, grab bars, lever handles."},
  example:"A 42-year-old shopkeeper in Erode with T10 paraplegia cannot enter his home because of three steps and a squat toilet. A ramp at 1:12, a widened bathroom door and a western commode with grab bars let him manage independently.",
  flags:["Ramp too steep (risk of tipping)","Insensate legs touching hot pipes","Loose rugs and poor lighting (falls)","Modification done without training the patient"],
  viva:[["Recommended ramp gradient for wheelchair users?","1:12 or gentler."],["Minimum turning space for a wheelchair?","About 1500 mm diameter."],["Which side should stair rails be for hemiplegia?","On the unaffected side at least, ideally both sides."]],
  refs:[{c:"Government of India, Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs. Harmonised Guidelines and Standards for Universal Accessibility in India, 2021."},{b:"osullivan",ch:"Environmental assessment"},{c:"Frontera WR (ed). DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. Chapter: Home and environmental modifications."}],
  keywords:"architectural barriers home modification ramp gradient accessibility universal design RA stroke SCI",
  mcq:[["The usually recommended maximum ramp gradient for independent wheelchair use is:",["1:4","1:6","1:12","1:2"],2,"A 1:12 slope is manageable for most wheelchair users."],["For a patient with RA, the best tap type is:",["Small round knob","Lever tap","Push-button high on wall","Screw tap"],1,"Levers avoid tight grip and twisting of small joints."]]
}
